{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2076 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "b059d7ef43271f4036a612199356cbe62545ed7760cf74ded82c3005d99dff79" :edb/db-before-t 2075 :edb/db-after-t 2076 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535388 50 #inst "2026-10-13T22:42:50.246Z" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186050012 1028 #edb/ref 17592186502066 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186050012 1028 #edb/ref 17592186502067 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186050012 1028 #edb/ref 17592186502068 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186050012 1028 #edb/ref 17592186502069 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186050012 1031 "Solving Problems Using Multiplication and Division of Rational Numbers" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186050012 1031 "Solving Problems Using the Four Operations on Rational Numbers" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186065721 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065723 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186065721 1075 #edb/ref 17592186065725 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186065721 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501522 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186065721 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501881 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186065721 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501522 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186068693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502140 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186068693 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502159 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186068694 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502150 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186068805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502073 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186068805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502106 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186069048 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186069048 1071 "$\\frac{7x+1}{7x}=$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186069048 1071 "What is $\\frac{7x + 1}{7x}$?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186069048 1074 "The numerator and denominator have no common factors, so the expression can not be reduced. The expression \n\n$$\n\\frac{7x+1}{7x}\n$$\n\n is already in lowest terms." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186084752 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084753 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186084752 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084754 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186084752 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084755 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186084752 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084756 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186084752 1075 #edb/ref 17592186084757 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186084752 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501636 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186084752 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501637 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186084752 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501769 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186084752 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501828 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186084752 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501902 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186084752 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501636 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186085129 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501655 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186085129 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501655 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186087433 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=cx+7$ is $c$.\n- The slope of the line $y=5x-3$ is $5$.\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $c=5$." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186087433 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=cx+7$ is $c.$\n\n- The slope of the line $y=5x-3$ is $5.$\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $c=5.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186087434 1071 "Which of the following lines is parallel to $12x - y=3$?\n\n1. $y=4x-8$\n2. $y=12x+1$\n3. $y=3x-12$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186087434 1071 "Which of the following lines is parallel to $12x - y=3?$\n\nI. $y=4x-8$\n\nII. $y=12x+1$\n\nIII. $y=3x-12$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186087434 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $12x-y=3$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n12x - y &= 3 \\\\\n- y &= -12x + 3 \\\\\ny &= 12x - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $12$.\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $12$ is\n\n$$\ny = 12x+1\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186087434 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $12x-y=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-y & =3 \\\\ -y & =-12x+3 \\\\ y & =12x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $12.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $12$ is $y = 12x+1.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186087435 1071 "The lines $y=2x$ and $kx-2y=-1$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186087435 1071 "The lines $y=2x$ and $kx-2y=-1$ are parallel. What is the value of $k?$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186087435 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y=2x$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $2$.\n- To find the slope of the line $kx-2y=-1$, we convert to slope-intercept form:\n$kx - 2y|=-1; - 2y|=-kx - 1; 2y|= kx + 1; y|= \\frac{k}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}$\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\dfrac{k}{2}$.\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{k}{2}=2\n$$\n\nConsequently, $k=4$." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186087435 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y=2x$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $2.$\n\n- To find the slope of the line $kx-2y=-1,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-2y & =-1 \\\\ -2y & =-kx-1 \\\\ 2y & =kx+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\dfrac{k}{2}.$\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have $\\dfrac{k}{2}=2.$ Consequently, $k=4.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186087686 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502083 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186087686 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502163 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186087687 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502109 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186087687 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502128 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186087687 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502137 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186087688 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502071 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186087688 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502088 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186087910 1050 420.0 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186088003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186088003 1071 "Which of the following lines is parallel to $8x - y = 2$?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186088003 1071 "Which of the following lines is parallel to $8x-y=2?$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186088003 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $8x-y=2,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-y & =2 \\\\ -y & =-8x+2 \\\\ y & =8x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $8.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $8$ is $y=8x-3.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186088068 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186088068 1071 "The lines $8x - 2y =-2$ and $kx - 5y = 15$ are parallel. What is the value of $k$?$k =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186088068 1071 "The lines $8x-2y=-2$ and $kx-5y=15$ are parallel. What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186088068 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $8x-2y=-2,$ we convert to slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-2y & =-2 \\\\ -2y & =-8x-2 \\\\ 2y & =8x+2 \\\\ y & =4x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $4.$\n\n- To find the slope of the line $kx-5y=15,$ we convert to slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-5y & =15 \\\\ -5y & =-kx+15 \\\\ 5y & =kx-15 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{5}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\frac{k}{5}.$\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have $\\frac{k}{5}=4.$ We solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{5} & =4 \\\\ k & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186088989 1064 "Two distinct lines that never intersect are said to be **parallel.** For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope. \n\nFor example, the lines $y=2x+4$ and $y=2x-5$ have the same slope of $m=2,$ so they are parallel. Indeed, looking at the graph, we see that the two lines never cross.\n\n![](images/c7/c7d36372d9646d0ce10eff5d35a4f9b8e56a7b37b333c5d53ac243065ee751af.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the lines $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+4$ and $y=2x-5$ have different slopes, so they are *not* parallel. Indeed, looking at the graph, we see that the two lines *do* cross.\n\n![](images/92/92c1ee39ccbbbf20dc3f4a7f76a1114d975fb0826eeac13d273b4b4fd9a83105.png)" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186088989 1064 "Two lines that never cross are said to be **parallel**. For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nFor example, the lines\n\n$$\ny=2x+4\n$$\n\nand $y=2x-5$ have the same slope of $m=2$, so they are parallel. Indeed, looking at the graph, we see that the two lines never cross.\n\n![](images/c7/c7d36372d9646d0ce10eff5d35a4f9b8e56a7b37b333c5d53ac243065ee751af.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the lines\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{1}{2}x+4\n$$\n\nand $y=2x-5$ have different slopes, so they are *not* parallel. Indeed, looking at the graph, we see that the two lines *do* cross.\n\n![](images/92/92c1ee39ccbbbf20dc3f4a7f76a1114d975fb0826eeac13d273b4b4fd9a83105.png)" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186089795 1078 #edb/ref 17592186089797 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092338 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502090 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092338 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502110 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092339 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502079 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092339 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502127 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092407 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093000 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186092407 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093001 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186092407 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093008 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186092407 1043 #edb/ref 17592186093178 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186092407 1043 #edb/ref 17592186502176 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092407 1043 #edb/ref 17592186502186 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092407 1043 #edb/ref 17592186502187 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092407 1043 #edb/ref 17592186502188 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092407 1043 #edb/ref 17592186502209 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092407 1043 #edb/ref 17592186502210 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092407 1045 "Solving Problems Using Multiplication and Division of Rational Numbers" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092407 1045 "Solving Problems Using the Four Operations on Rational Numbers" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186092407 1046 #edb/ref 17592186093001 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186092407 1046 #edb/ref 17592186502186 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092407 1050 1440.0 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092604 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092604 1071 "$$\n{1}_{A}(5)=1,\\,{1}_{B}(5)=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=0$\n\nIII. $5\\in A\\cup B$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186092604 1071 "$1_{A}(5) = 1, 1_{B}(5) = 0$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $1_{A∪B}(5) = 1$\n2. $1_{A∪B}(5) = 0$\n3. $5 ∈ A∪B$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186092604 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(5),{1}_{B}(5)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{1,0\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=1,$ we conclude that $5$ belongs to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186093000 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093178 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186093001 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093000 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186093178 1058 #edb/ref 17592186093008 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186093496 1064 "Recall that the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n![](images/74/7408ad6ce2700ac59d8e6a78c511ba2b51551e6b9d039ee7e2f8548f4177802e.svg)\n\nTherefore, the indicator function of the complement $\\overline{A}$ is given by \n\n![](images/de/de1a51c00e16d836fde350e0936fb9cb63124c72b49174210be3e6f6faa152da.svg)\n\nThis means that the indicator function of $A$ and its complement are related as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the indicator functions of $A \\cap B$ and union $A \\cup B$ can be expressed through the indicator functions of $A$ and $B.$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *intersection*, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *union*, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)+{1}_{B}(x)-{1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can prove these results by splitting them into cases." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186093496 1064 "Recall that the indicator function of a set $A \\subseteq X$ is the function\n\n![](images/74/7408ad6ce2700ac59d8e6a78c511ba2b51551e6b9d039ee7e2f8548f4177802e.svg)\n\nTherefore, the indicator function of the complement $\\overline{A}$ is given by \n\n![](images/de/de1a51c00e16d836fde350e0936fb9cb63124c72b49174210be3e6f6faa152da.svg)\n\nThis means that the indicator function of $A$ and its complement are related as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the indicator functions of $A \\cap B$ and union $A \\cup B$ can be expressed through the indicator functions of $A$ and $B.$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *intersection*, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the indicator function of the *union*, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)} \\\\  & ={1}_{A}(x)+{1}_{B}(x)-{1}_{A}(x)\\cdot {1}_{B}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can prove these results by splitting them into cases." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186098879 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501508 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186098879 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501508 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186101009 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502153 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186101010 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502126 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186101258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186101258 1071 "![](images/05/051c906be77d7e12a778449883a630c3b24f92b84ae7dac3d93b1787824f9c0c.png)\n\nConsider the line shown above, which has a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}$. The equation of the line is $y =$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186101258 1071 "![](images/05/051c906be77d7e12a778449883a630c3b24f92b84ae7dac3d93b1787824f9c0c.png)\n\nConsider the line shown above, which has a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$ The equation of the line is $y=${{field-1}}." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186101258 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ We know that the slope is $m=\\frac{2}{3},$ so we just need to work out $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line passes through the point $(3,3).$ Substituting this into $y=mx+b$ with $m=\\frac{2}{3}$ and solving for $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+b \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{2}{3}(3)+b \\\\ 3 & =2+b \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+1.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186110005 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110006 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186110005 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110007 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186110005 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110008 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186110005 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110009 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186110005 1075 #edb/ref 17592186110010 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186110005 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501587 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186110005 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501704 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186110005 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501742 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186110005 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501762 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186110005 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501886 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186110005 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501587 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186110616 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502081 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186110616 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502113 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186110616 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502124 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186111380 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186111380 1071 "${(\\frac{6}{{4}^{2}})}^{3}=$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186111380 1071 "What is $(\\frac{6}{4^{2}})^{3}$?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186111380 1074 "First, we apply the power of quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{6}{{4}^{2}})}^{3}=\\frac{{6}^{3}}{{({4}^{2})}^{3}}\n$$\n\nTo simplify the denominator, we use the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{6}^{3}}{{({4}^{2})}^{3}} & =\\frac{{6}^{3}}{{4}^{2\\cdot 3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{3}}{{4}^{6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186113682 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113683 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186113682 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113684 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186113682 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113685 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186113682 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113686 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186113682 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113687 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186113682 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501633 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186113682 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501736 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186113682 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501800 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186113682 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501884 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186113682 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501973 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186113682 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501633 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186113688 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501626 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186113688 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501626 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186115373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502115 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186115373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502122 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186115373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502158 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186115373 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502161 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186115374 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502100 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186115375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502130 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186115375 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502157 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186115463 1050 1500.0 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186115790 1071 "A tank initially holds $450L$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $9kg$ of salt.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $21L/min$, while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $24L/min$. Which differential equation best describes the amount of salt $Q$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the fresh water begins to be added?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186115790 1071 "A tank initially holds $450\\,\\text{L}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $21\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of salt $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the fresh water begins to be added?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186115790 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $21\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (21\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $450\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Fresh water enters the tank at a rate of $21\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $21t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $24t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n450+21t-24t=(450-3t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24Q}{450-3t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{150-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{8Q}{150-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{8}{150-t}Q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186115791 1071 "A tank initially holds $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind an equation for the amount of salt $Q(t),$ in pounds, in the tank $t$ minutes after the fresh water is first added.\n\n$Q(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186115791 1071 "A tank initially holds $120gal$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $20lb$ of salt.\nFresh water is poured into the tank at a rate of $4gal/min$, while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $4gal/min$.\nFind an equation for the amount of salt $Q(t)$, in pounds, in the tank $t$ minutes after the fresh water is first added.$Q(t) =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186115791 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that fresh water (i.e., a brine solution containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNote that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume of water in the tank remains $120\\,\\text{gal}.$\nThen, since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{120}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{30}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =0-\\frac{Q}{30} \\\\ \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\,Q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{30} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{30})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{30}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-t/30+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/30},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $20\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=20,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =20 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/30} & =20 \\\\ A & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=20{e}^{-t/30}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186116425 1064 "Consider a container which initially holds $V_0\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution containing $a\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of a substance.\n\n- Another solution, containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of the substance per gallon, flows *into* the container at the rate $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$\n\n- At the same time, the completely mixed solution flows *out* of the container at the rate $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\text{min}.$\n\n![](images/31/312d11bb0a00f6f668b653b00a7eecda55cdb23c5a7c09076734774b7c05a50b.png)\n\nHow can we model the amount $Q$ (in pounds) of substance in the container at time $t?$\n\nTo model $Q(t),$ we can analyze how it changes over time.\nThe rate of change of the amount of substance in the container is given by \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out},\n$$\n\n where:\n\n- $Q'_\\text{in}$ is the rate at which the substance *enters* the container. A solution containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}$ of the substance enters the container at a rate of $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (e\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (e\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =be\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $Q'_\\text{out}$ is the rate at which the substance *leaves* the container. To determine the rate of exit, we first find the volume of solution in the container at time $t.$\n\nStarting from the initial volume $V_0,$ we add the volume that enters, $et,$ and subtract the volume that leaves, $ft.$ Thus, the volume of solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n(V_0 + et - ft)\\,\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of substance in the container, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}.\n$$\n\n So, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min},$ the substance leaves the container at the rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(f\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(f\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft})\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = be - f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right)\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right) = be}\n$$\n\nLet's see this in a more concrete example." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186116425 1064 "Consider a container which initially holds $V_0\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution containing $a\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of a substance.\n\n- Another solution, containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of the substance per gallon, flows *into* the container at the rate $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$\n- At the same time, the completely mixed solution flows *out* of the container at the rate $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\text{min}.$\n\n![](images/31/312d11bb0a00f6f668b653b00a7eecda55cdb23c5a7c09076734774b7c05a50b.png)\n\nHow can we model the amount $Q$ (in pounds) of substance in the container at time $t?$\n\nTo model $Q(t),$ we can analyze how it changes over time. The rate of change of the amount of substance in the container is given by $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_\\text{in} - Q'_\\text{out},$$ where:\n\n- $Q'_\\text{in}$ is the rate at which the substance *enters* the container. A solution containing $b\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}$ of the substance enters the container at a rate of $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min}.$ So, we have $$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{'} & =(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (e\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =(b\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (e\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =be\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n- $Q'_\\text{out}$ is the rate at which the substance *leaves* the container. To determine the rate of exit, we first find the volume of solution in the container at time $t.$\n\nStarting from the initial volume $V_0,$ we add the volume that enters, $et,$ and subtract the volume that leaves, $ft.$ Thus, the volume of solution at time $t$ is $$(V_0 + et - ft)\\,\\textrm{gal}.$$ Since $Q$ is the mass of substance in the container, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is $$\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}.$$ So, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min},$ the substance leaves the container at the rate of $$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{'} & =(f\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\ & =(f\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\ & =f(\\frac{Q}{{V}_{0}+et-ft})\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = be - f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right) \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + et - ft}\\right) = be}$$\n\nLet's see this in a more concrete example." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186116426 1064 "Suppose that a tank initially holds $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant.\nA solution containing no contaminant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nLet's find a differential equation that describes the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the solution begins to be added.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dissolved contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nrepresents the rate at which this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of dissolved contaminant enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Solution enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n450 + 8t - 8t = 450\\,\\textrm{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved contaminant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{Q}{450}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *dissolved contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{225}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{4Q}{225}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{225}Q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186116426 1064 "Suppose that a tank initially holds $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant. A solution containing no contaminant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nLet's find a differential equation that describes the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the solution begins to be added.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dissolved contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$$ represents the rate at which this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank:\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = Q'_{\\textrm{in}} - Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t}, Q'_{\\textrm{in}}$ and $Q'_{\\textrm{out}}$ are $\\textrm{kg}/\\textrm{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0\\,\\textrm{kg/L}$ of dissolved contaminant enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved contaminant* enters the tank is\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{'} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\ & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved contaminant exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of solution.\n- Solution enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have entered the tank.\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $8t\\,\\textrm{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is $$450 + 8t - 8t = 450\\,\\textrm{L}.$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved contaminant in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is $$\\dfrac{Q}{450}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{kg}}{\\textrm{L}}.$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ *dissolved contaminant* leaves at a rate of $$\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{'} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\ & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\ & =\\frac{8Q}{450}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\ & =\\frac{4Q}{225}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{'}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{'} \\\\ & =0-\\frac{4Q}{225}\\end{aligned}$$ which finally gives\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{225}Q=0.\\end{aligned}$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186116427 1064 "Previously, we saw that the volume $V$ of solution at time $t$ in a container with initial volume $V_0,$ input rate $e,$ and output rate $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0 + (e - f)t.\n$$\n\nThere are three possible behaviors for the volume depending on the rates:\n\n- The volume *remains constant* when the input rate equals the output rate, $e=f.$ In this case, \n\n$$\nV(t) = V_0.\n$$\n\n- The volume *decreases* when the output rate is greater than the input rate, $f > e > 0,$ and the container begins to empty. Let $t_e$ be the time when the container empties: $V(t_e) = 0.$ Solving for $t_e,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{e}) & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e} \\\\ 0 & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the container empties at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_e = \\dfrac{V_0}{f - e}}.$\n\n- The volume *increases* when the input rate is greater than the output rate, $e > f > 0,$ and the container begins filling until it reaches its capacity $V_\\text{cap}.$ Let $t_o$ be the time when the container reaches capacity: $V(t_o) = V_\\text{cap}.$ Solving for $t_o,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & ={V}_{\\text{cap}} \\\\ {V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{o} & ={V}_{\\text{cap}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the container overflows at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_o = \\dfrac{V_\\text{cap} - V_0}{e - f}}.$\n\nNow, let's apply one of these formulas in a concrete example." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186116427 1064 "Previously, we saw that the volume $V$ of solution at time $t$ in a container with initial volume $V_0,$ input rate $e,$ and output rate $f$ is given by\n\n$$V(t) = V_0 + (e - f)t.$$\n\nThere are three possible behaviors for the volume depending on the rates:\n\n- The volume *remains constant* when the input rate equals the output rate, $e=f.$ In this case, $$V(t) = V_0.$$\n- The volume *decreases* when the output rate is greater than the input rate, $f > e > 0,$ and the container begins to empty. Let $t_e$ be the time when the container empties: $V(t_e) = 0.$ Solving for $t_e,$ we get $$\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{e}) & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e} \\\\ 0 & ={V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{e}.\\end{aligned}$$ Solving this equation, we find that the container empties at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_e = \\dfrac{V_0}{f - e}}.$\n\n- The volume *increases* when the input rate is greater than the output rate, $e > f > 0,$ and the container begins filling until it reaches its capacity $V_\\text{cap}.$ Let $t_o$ be the time when the container reaches capacity: $V(t_o) = V_\\text{cap}.$ Solving for $t_o,$ we get $$\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & ={V}_{\\text{cap}} \\\\ {V}_{0}+(e-f){t}_{o} & ={V}_{\\text{cap}}.\\end{aligned}$$ Solving this equation, we find that the container overflows at time $\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid #d3d3d3]{t_o = \\dfrac{V_\\text{cap} - V_0}{e - f}}.$\n\nNow, let's apply one of these formulas in a concrete example." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186116428 1064 "So far, we have seen that if a container initially holds $V_0\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution containing $a\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of a substance, and\n\n- a solution with concentration $b\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}$ flows into the container at the rate $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min},$ while\n\n- the completely mixed solution flows out at the rate $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\text{min},$\n\nthen the amount $Q(t)$ of the substance in the container satisfies the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\\right) = be.\n$$\n\nFor the rest of this lesson, we'll focus on the special case where the input solution contains *no substance*, that is, where $b=0.$ In this case, the right-hand side vanishes, and the differential equation becomes *separable*:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\\right) = 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac1Q\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = - \\dfrac{f}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is negative, meaning the amount of substance in the container decreases over time. We can now solve the differential equation directly by separation of variables.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider our previous example: a tank initially holds $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant, and a solution containing no contaminant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nWe found that the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank after $t$ minutes is modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{4}{225}Q = 0\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{1}{Q} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{4}{225}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by integrating both sides with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}\\,dt & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{225})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\,dQ & =-\\frac{4}{225}\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{4t}{225}+C \\\\ Q & ={e}^{-4t/225+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-4t/225}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nFinally, since the tank initially contains $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-4(0)/225} & =6 \\\\ A & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{Q(t) = 6e^{-4t/225}}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186116428 1064 "So far, we have seen that if a container initially holds $V_0\\,\\textrm{gal}$ of solution containing $a\\,\\textrm{lb}$ of a substance, and\n\n- a solution with concentration $b\\,\\textrm{lb}/\\textrm{gal}$ flows into the container at the rate $e\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\textrm{min},$ while\n- the completely mixed solution flows out at the rate $f\\,\\textrm{gal}/\\text{min},$\n\nthen the amount $Q(t)$ of the substance in the container satisfies the differential equation\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\\right) = be.$$\n\nFor the rest of this lesson, we'll focus on the special case where the input solution contains *no substance*, that is, where $b=0.$ In this case, the right-hand side vanishes, and the differential equation becomes *separable*:\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + f\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{V_0 + (e-f)t}\\right) = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac1Q\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = - \\dfrac{f}{V_0 + (e-f)t}$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side is negative, meaning the amount of substance in the container decreases over time. We can now solve the differential equation directly by separation of variables.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider our previous example: a tank initially holds $450\\,\\textrm{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant, and a solution containing no contaminant is pumped into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\textrm{L/min}.$\n\nWe found that the amount of dissolved contaminant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank after $t$ minutes is modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{4}{225}Q = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{1}{Q} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}Q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -\\dfrac{4}{225}.$$\n\nWe solve this equation by integrating both sides with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}\\,dt & =\\int (-\\frac{4}{225})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\,dQ & =-\\frac{4}{225}\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|Q| & =-\\frac{4t}{225}+C \\\\ Q & ={e}^{-4t/225+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-4t/225}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNote that $A = \\pm e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nFinally, since the tank initially contains $6\\,\\textrm{kg}$ of dissolved contaminant, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-4(0)/225} & =6 \\\\ A & =6\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t){:}$\n\n$$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{Q(t) = 6e^{-4t/225}}$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186117747 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117748 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186117747 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117749 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186117747 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117750 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186117747 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117751 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186117747 1075 #edb/ref 17592186117752 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186117747 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501510 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186117747 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501686 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186117747 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501774 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186117747 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501783 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186117747 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501792 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186117747 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501792 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186119965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502070 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186119965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502094 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186119965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502145 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186119965 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502162 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186119966 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502089 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186120028 1050 480.0 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186120267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117741 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186120268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186120268 1071 "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $Cy = e^{2x}$, where $C \\ne 0$ and $K$ are parameters." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186120268 1071 "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $Cy={e}^{2x},$ where $C\\ne 0$ and $K$ are parameters." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186120268 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $Cy={e}^{2x}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(Cy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}({e}^{2x}) \\\\ C{y}^{′} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must eliminate $C.$ From the original family of curves equation, we have\n\n$$\nCy={e}^{2x}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our slope equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{y})}=2y.\n$$\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{2y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{1}{2y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-1 \\\\ \\int 2y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-1)\\,dx \\\\ \\int 2y\\,dy & =-\\int dx \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x+K \\\\ {y}^{2}+x & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $Cy={e}^{2x},$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+x=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e9/e941d35788f4aee837220962511ca537528b04dec9efaa7f956a4ee571e14189.png)\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ we have $y\\ne 0$ on every curve in the family, so slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186120982 1064 "Recall that a *one-parameter family* of curves in the $xy$-plane is defined by\n\n$$\nF(x,y,C) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ denotes the parameter.\n\nThe **orthogonal trajectories** of a one-parameter family of curves form another one-parameter family of curves such that every curve in one family intersects at right angles every curve in the other.\n\nFor example, circles $x^2+y^2=C^2$ (with parameter $C$) and lines through the origin $y=Kx$ (with parameter $K$) are orthogonal trajectories.\n\n![](images/ca/ca980af3c5db940a9ab95567dfaa1a69ea8afb9f4f42f772bb446df67df129b8.png)\n\nNow, let $F(x,y,C) = 0$ be a family of curves with parameter $C.$ Suppose we can\n\n- implicitly differentiate it with respect to $x,$ and\n\n- eliminate $C$ using the derived and defining equations,\n\nto get an equation in terms of $x,$ $y,$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$  Next, assume we can solve for the derivative and obtain a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = f(x,y)\n$$\n\nthat explicitly gives the slopes of the curves satisfying this ODE (at least locally, where the slope is defined). \n\nFor a curve to intersect at right angles with every curve in the family, its slope must be the negative reciprocal of the slope of curves in the family. So, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves must be the solutions of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = -\\dfrac{1}{f(x,y)}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** This is true only where $f(x,y)\\neq 0.$ If $f=0$, then original curves have slope $0$ (horizontal tangent), and orthogonal curves should have infinite slope (vertical tangent)." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186120982 1064 "Recall that a *one-parameter family* of curves in the $xy$-plane is defined by $$F(x,y,C) = 0,$$ where $C$ denotes the parameter.\n\nThe **orthogonal trajectories** of a one-parameter family of curves form another one-parameter family of curves such that every curve in one family intersects at right angles every curve in the other.\n\nFor example, circles $x^2+y^2=C^2$ (with parameter $C$) and lines through the origin $y=Kx$ (with parameter $K$) are orthogonal trajectories.\n\n![](images/ca/ca980af3c5db940a9ab95567dfaa1a69ea8afb9f4f42f772bb446df67df129b8.png)\n\nNow, let $F(x,y,C) = 0$ be a family of curves with parameter $C.$ Suppose we can\n\n- implicitly differentiate it with respect to $x,$ and\n- eliminate $C$ using the derived and defining equations,\n\nto get an equation in terms of $x,$ $y,$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ Next, assume we can solve for the derivative and obtain a differential equation of the form $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = f(x,y)$$ that explicitly gives the slopes of the curves satisfying this ODE (at least locally, where the slope is defined).\n\nFor a curve to intersect at right angles with every curve in the family, its slope must be the negative reciprocal of the slope of curves in the family. So, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves must be the solutions of $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = -\\dfrac{1}{f(x,y)}.$$\n\n**Watch out!** This is true only where $f(x,y)\\neq 0.$ If $f=0$, then original curves have slope $0$ (horizontal tangent), and orthogonal curves should have infinite slope (vertical tangent)." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186120985 1064 "Suppose we want to determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy^3 = C^2,$ where $C\\neq0$ is a parameter.\n\nOrthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $xy^3 = C^2.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(x{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ {y}^{3}+3x{y}^{2}{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3x{y}^{2}{y}^{′} & =-{y}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope $y'_\\perp$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope $y'$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\ny'_\\perp = -\\dfrac{1}{y'} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(-\\dfrac{y}{3x}\\right)}\n= \\dfrac{3x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{3x}{y}}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C \\neq 0,$ the given family has $x\\neq0$ and $y\\neq0,$ so both $y'$ and $y'_\\perp$ are well-defined." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186120985 1064 "Suppose we want to determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy^3 = C^2,$ where $C\\neq0$ is a parameter.\n\nOrthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $xy^3 = C^2.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(x{y}^{3}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ {y}^{3}+3x{y}^{2}{y}^{'} & =0 \\\\ 3x{y}^{2}{y}^{'} & =-{y}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{'} & =-\\frac{y}{3x}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope $y'_\\perp$ must be the negative reciprocal of