ed too." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186184098 1074 "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=-2,$ we have $y=-2.$ So, $f(-2)=-2.$\n\n- When $x=1,$ the function is **undefined** because the function has an open circle at this point.\n\nTherefore, since $f(1)$ is undefined, the sum $f(-2)+f(1)$ is undefined too." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184099 1071 "What is the graph of $y = f(x)$, where $f(x) = 2x - 3$?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186184099 1071 "What is the graph of $y = f(x),$ where $f(x) = 2x - 3?$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184099 1074 "Recall that the graph of $y = 2x - 3$ is a straight line, and two distinct points can determine any straight line.\n\nSo, given any two distinct values of $x$, we can find the corresponding $y$-values by substituting $x$ into the formula $y = 2x - 3$.\nFor example,\n\n- if $x = 0$ then $y = 2(0) - 3 = -3$, which gives the point $(0,-3)$, and\n- if $x = 1$ then $y = 2(1) - 3 = -1$, which gives the point $(1,-1)$.\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs in the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This gives the following graph.\n\n![](images/9f/9f3005c9fac13668b126396f11aa8f3141d6de63d6e88e90d270901a9253fbd1.png)" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186184099 1074 "Recall that the graph of $y = 2x - 3$ is a straight line, and two distinct points can determine any straight line.\n\nSo, given any two distinct values of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$-values by substituting $x$ into the formula $y = 2x - 3.$\nFor example,\n\n- if $x = 0$ then $y = 2(0) - 3 = -3,$ which gives the point $(0,-3),$ and\n\n- if $x = 1$ then $y = 2(1) - 3 = -1,$ which gives the point $(1,-1).$\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs in the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This gives the following graph.\n\n![](images/9f/9f3005c9fac13668b126396f11aa8f3141d6de63d6e88e90d270901a9253fbd1.png)" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184145 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502077 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184145 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502099 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184146 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502121 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184147 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502080 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184147 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502091 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184201 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502076 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184201 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502084 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184201 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502125 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184202 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502143 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184202 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502144 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184361 1050 540.0 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184406 1050 420.0 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184545 1074 "To determine the amount of flour that Nancy needs, we need to calculate $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 6.$\n\nFirst, we write  $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $6$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n6=\\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{6}{1}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($4$) and the second fraction's numerator ($6$) have a common \nfactor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{6}{1} & =\\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\times 3}{1\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{2}=13\\,\\text{R}\\,1=13\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, Nancy needs $13\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ cups of flour." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184809 1071 "Fred is $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{ft}$ tall. Julie is $\\frac{4}{5}$ of Fred's height. How tall is Julie?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184809 1071 "Fred is $4\\frac{1}{2}ft$ tall. Julie is $\\frac{4}{5}$ of Fred's height. How tall is Julie?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186184809 1074 "To find out Julie's height, we need to calculate $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFirst, we write $4\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(4\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($2$) and the second fraction's numerator ($4$) have a common \nfactor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{9}{5}\\times \\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}\\times \\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\times 2}{5\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting this to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n18÷5=3\\,\\text{R}\\,3=3\\,\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, Julie is $3\\,\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{ft}$ tall." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184818 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184818 1071 "A box of cornflakes cereal weighs $1\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{oz}.$ Last week, Debby ate $2$ full boxes and another box which was only $\\frac{1}{4}$ full. How many ounces of this cereal did Debby eat in total?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186184818 1071 "A box of cornflakes cereal weighs $1\\frac{1}{2}oz$. Last week, Debby ate $2$ full boxes and another box which was only $\\frac{1}{4}$ full. How many ounces of this cereal did Debbie eat in total?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186184818 1074 "Notice that Debby had $2+\\frac{1}{4}=2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ boxes of cornflakes. So, to find out the total weight, we need to calculate $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times 2\\,\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nFirst, we write  $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(1\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{9}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 9}{2\\times 8}=\\frac{27}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{8}=3\\,\\text{R}\\,3=3\\,\\frac{3}{8}\n$$\n\nSo, Debby ate a total of $3\\,\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{oz}$ of cornflakes cereal." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186185323 1064 "We can plot a function $f(x)$ by setting $y=f(x)$.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2-1\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is any number taken from the set $\\{-2, -1, 0, 1,2 \\}$.\n\nFirst, we create a table containing the input values:\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nGiven a value of $x$, we can find the corresponding $y$-value by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=x^2-1$. For example,\n\n- if $x=-2$ then $y=(-2)^2-1=3$,\n- if $x=-1$ then $y=(-1)^2-1=0$,\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in the same way, we get the following values:\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs in the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/06/0623f7a3756288a982b2e26dbbf03f6ed924f2cdea495764bc78ed547efbbcc3.png)\n\nWe don't connect the points since $f(x)$ is defined only for values of $x$ contained within the set $\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}$.\n\nLet's now take a look at an example where $x$ is unrestricted." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186185323 1064 "We can plot a function $f(x)$ by setting $y=f(x).$\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the function \n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2-1,\n$$\n\n where $x$ is any number taken from the set $\\{-2, -1, 0, 1,2 \\}.$\n\nFirst, we create a table containing the input values:\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nGiven a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$-value by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=x^2-1.$ For example,\n\n- if $x=-2$ then $y=(-2)^2-1=3,$\n\n- if $x=-1$ then $y=(-1)^2-1=0,$\n\n- $\\cdots$\n\nContinuing in the same way, we get the following values:\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs in the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/06/0623f7a3756288a982b2e26dbbf03f6ed924f2cdea495764bc78ed547efbbcc3.png)\n\nWe don't connect the points since $f(x)$ is defined only for values of $x$ contained within the set $\\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}.$ \n\nLet's now take a look at an example where $x$ is unrestricted." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186185324 1064 "Consider the function $y=f(x)$, shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa86c914aeedc2ff973d22228fb11902aa7246fa751bddb75746e517d97fa60b.png)\n\nWe can determine the values of this function at particular points by reading them off its graph.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(-2,3)$, we know that $f(-2) = 3$.\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(-1,0)$, we know that $f(-1) = 0$.\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(0,-1)$, we know that $f(0) = -1$.\n\nNotice that the point $(2,3)$ is shown with an empty circle. This means that the function is **undefined** at the point where $x=2$." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186185324 1064 "Consider the function $y=f(x),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa86c914aeedc2ff973d22228fb11902aa7246fa751bddb75746e517d97fa60b.png)\n\nWe can determine the values of this function at particular points by reading them off its graph. \n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(-2,3),$ we know that $f(-2) = 3.$\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(-1,0),$ we know that $f(-1) = 0.$\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(0,-1),$ we know that $f(0) = -1.$\n\nNotice that the point $(2,3)$ is shown with an empty circle. This means that the function is **undefined** at the point where $x=2.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186185379 1064 "Multiplication of mixed fractions occurs regularly in real-life situations.\n\nFor example, suppose that a country road is\n\n$$\n5 \\, \\dfrac 12\\,\\textrm{km}\n$$\n\nlong. If\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 35\n$$\n\nof it is paved with asphalt, how long is the paved part?\n\nTo find out the length of the paved part of the road, we need to calculate\n\n$$\n5 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nFirst, we write\n\n$$\n5 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n5 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{(5 \\times 2) + 1}{2} = \\dfrac{11}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{2} \\times \\dfrac{3}{5} = \\dfrac{11 \\times 3}{2 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{33}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, by converting this fraction to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n33 \\div 10 = 3\\,\\textrm{R}\\,3 = 3\\,\\dfrac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nSo, the paved part of the road is\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac{3}{10}\\,\\textrm{km}\n$$\n\nlong." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186185379 1064 "Multiplication of mixed fractions occurs regularly in real-life situations.\n\nFor example, suppose that a country road is $5 \\, \\dfrac 12\\,\\textrm{km}$ long. If $\\dfrac 35$ of it is paved with asphalt, how long is the paved part?  \n\nTo find out the length of the paved part of the road, we need to calculate $5 \\, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{3}{5}.$ \n\nFirst, we write $5 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n5 \\,\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{(5 \\times 2) + 1}{2} = \\dfrac{11}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{2} \\times \\dfrac{3}{5} =  \\dfrac{11 \\times 3}{2 \\times 5} = \\dfrac{33}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, by converting this fraction to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n33 \\div 10 = 3\\,\\textrm{R}\\,3 = 3\\,\\dfrac{3}{10}.\n$$\n\nSo, the paved part of the road is $3\\,\\dfrac{3}{10}\\,\\textrm{km}$ long." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186185604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185605 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186185604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185606 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186185604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185607 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186185604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185608 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186185604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185609 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186185604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501540 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186185604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501566 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186185604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501576 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186185604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501582 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186185604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501798 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186185604 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501582 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186185610 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185611 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186185610 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185612 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186185610 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185613 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186185610 1075 #edb/ref 17592186185614 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [1759218618