's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =120 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 20\\,000 \\\\  & =1\\,000\\,✓ \\\\ np & =120(0.75) \\\\  & =90 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =120(1-0.75) \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =25, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =10 \\\\  & <25.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5\\%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied. \n\nFor the other conditions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =45(0.9) \\\\  & =40.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =45(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4.5 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p) \\ngtr 5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186188193 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $n\\leq 5\\% \\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n- $np > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n$n|= 120; \\le 5% \\cdot N; = 0.05 \\cdot 20000; = 1000✓; np|= 120(0.75); = 90; > 5✓; n(1 - p)|= 120(1 - 0.75); = 30; > 5.✓$\nTherefore, we c