 $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=120\\,\\text{g},\\,n=81,\\,s=18\\,\\text{g}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{80,0.025}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{80,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=81-1=80$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/69/69f7cbb0e67e69c2ef95f9033f41ecd37a0ed21de94bcb903af17284aeb01155.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{80,0.005}=2.639:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $79$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.374$ | $2.640$ |\n| $80$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.374$ | $2.639$ |\n| $81$ | $1.292$ | $1.664$ | $1.990$ | $2.373$ | $2.638$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & \\pm 2.639\\cdot \\frac{18}{\\sqrt{81}}\\,\\text{g} \\\\ 120 & \\pm 5.278\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186188555 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186188555 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n = 9$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $42$ and the sample standard deviation is $6$, find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $μ$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 3.355) = 0.005$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question