.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.753)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.05}=1.753.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 50\\pm 1.753\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & 50\\pm 3.506\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186188681 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186188681 1071 "In a school of $900$ students, $432$ of the students are male. If the school randomly samples $50$ students, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $20$ and $23$ (inclusive) of students in the sample are male. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1131\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1112\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1093\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1335\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1314\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1292\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1539\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1515\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3557\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3520\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3483\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3936\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3897\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3859\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4325\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4286\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4247\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186188681 1071 "In a school of $900$ students, $432$ of the students are male. If the school randomly samples $50$ students, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $20$ and $23$ (inclusive) of students in the sample are male. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$$0.01$$0.02$$0.03$$-1.2$ $0.1131$ $0.1112$ $0.1093$ $-1.1$ $0.1335$ $0.1314$ $0.1292$ $-1.0$ $0.1562$ $0.1539$ $0.1515$ |  | $z$$0.07$$0.08$$0.09$$-0.3$ $0.3557$ $0.3520$ $0.3483$ $-0.2$ $0.3936$ $0.3897$ $0.3859$ $-0.1$ $0.4325$ $0.4286$ $0.4247$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186188681 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=50,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{432}{900}=0.48.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=50(0.48)=24>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=50(1-0.48)=26>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=50$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.48,\\frac{0.48(1-0.48)}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.48,0.0050).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $20$ and $23$ of students in the sample are male, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{50}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{23}{50} \\\\ 0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.46).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.46) & =P(\\frac{0.4-0.48}{\\sqrt{0.0050}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.46-0.48}{\\sqrt{0.0050}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.13\\le Z\\le -0.28) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.28)-P(Z\\le -1.13) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.28)-Φ(-1.13)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.28)=0.3897,\\,Φ(-1.13)=0.1292.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.46) & =Φ(-0.28)-Φ(-1.13) \\\\  & =0.3897-0.1292 \\\\  & =0.2605.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186188685 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186188685 1071 "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=16$ from a population of size $N=100$ in which $25%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=32$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $10%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=32$  from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $75%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186188685 1071 "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $p^(^)$ be approximated as\n\n$p^(^)∼N(p, (p(1 - p))/(n))$,\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\n1. A sample of size $n = 16$ from a population of size $N = 100$ in which $25%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n2. A sample of size $n = 32$ from a population of size $N = 1000$ in which $10%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n3. A sample of size $n = 32$ from a population of size $N = 1000$ in which $75%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186188685 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =16,\\, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 100 \\\\  & =5 \\\\  & <16.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =32 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =32(0.1) \\\\  & =3.2 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =32 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =32(0.75) \\\\  & =24 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =32(1-0.75) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186189369 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a normal population with unknown population mean $\\mu$ and *known* population variance $\\sigma^2$. Recall that a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the sample mean,\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nOne thing to realize is that this situation is unrealistic: it's doubtful that we'll not know the population mean yet know the population variance.\n\nA more realistic situation is that $\\sigma^2$ is *unknown*, and we must estimate it using the sample data. To estimate the population variance $\\sigma^2$, we use the sample variance $S^2$, given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left(X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n} \\sim T_{n-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $T_{n-1}$ is a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186189369 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a normal population with unknown population mean $\\mu$ and *known* population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Recall that a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the sample mean, $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nOne thing to realize is that this situation is unrealistic: it's doubtful that we'll not know the population mean yet know the population variance.\n\nA more realistic situation is that $\\sigma^2$ is *unknown*, and we must estimate it using the sample data. To estimate the population variance $\\sigma^2,$ we use the sample variance $S^2,$ given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2.\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n} \\sim T_{n-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $T_{n-1}$ is a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186189370 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population with the unknown population mean $\\mu$ and sample variance $s^2$. Then, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x} +t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{n-1, \\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $T \\sim T_{n-1}$.\n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are called **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1, \\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean\n- $\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the standard error of the mean\n- $t_{n-1,\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $t$-score\n- $E = t_{n-1, \\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n$$\nt\n$$\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n(x^(―) - [margin of error],\\begin{vmatrix}x^(―) + [margin of error]) \\\\ (x^(―) - [[math]t[/math]-score] \\cdot [standard error], \\mid x^(―) + [[math]t[/math]-score] \\cdot [standard error]) \\\\ (x^(―) - t_{n - 1, α/2} \\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\\end{vmatrix}x^(―) + t_{n - 1, α/2} \\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}})\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression for the confidence interval is very similar to when the population variance was known. The only difference is that we now use the $t$-scores instead of $z$-scores and the sample standard deviation $s$ instead of the population standard deviation $\\sigma$.\n\nFinally, since computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186189370 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population with the unknown population mean $\\mu$ and sample variance $s^2.$ Then, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x} +t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T > t_{n-1, \\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $T \\sim T_{n-1}.$ \n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are called **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1, \\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean\n\n- $\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the standard error of the mean\n\n- $t_{n-1,\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $t$-score\n\n- $E = t_{n-1, \\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/10/10cd4a425a9668611335a7e80561944d147faaa9152f91fb9ed87378e8c092eb.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{[math]t[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{[math]t[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}, & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression for the confidence interval is very similar to when the population variance was known. The only difference is that we now use the $t$-scores instead of $z$-scores and the sample standard deviation $s$ instead of the population standard deviation $\\sigma.$\n\nFinally, since computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186189394 1064 "Up to now, we've assumed that the underlying population is normally distributed. However, if we have a *sufficiently large* sample, then according to the central limit theorem, we have the approximation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\approx N(0,1)\n$$\n\nMoreover, since $n$ is sufficiently large, $\\sigma^2$ can be approximated as $S^2$. Therefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n} \\approx T_{n-1}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If the population is *not* normally distributed, then the sample size $n$ must be sufficiently large for this approximation to apply. Typically,\n\n$$\nn\\geq 30\n$$\n\nis sufficient.\n\nIn such situations, we can use our formula for the confidence limits of the population mean $\\mu$:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186189394 1064 "Up to now, we've assumed that the underlying population is normally distributed. However, if we have a *sufficiently large* sample, then according to the central limit theorem, we have the approximation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\approx N(0,1).\n$$\n\nMoreover, since $n$ is sufficiently large, $\\sigma^2$ can be approximated as $S^2.$ Therefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n} \\approx T_{n-1}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If the population is *not* normally distributed, then the sample size $n$ must be sufficiently large for this approximation to apply. Typically, $n\\geq 30$ is sufficient.\n\nIn such situations, we can use our formula for the confidence limits of the population mean $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm t_{n-1,\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186189395 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=9$ drawn from a normal population. The sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=30\n$$\n\nand the sample standard deviation is $s=3.3$.\nWe wish to compute a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$.\n\nRecall that a confidence interval is an interval of the form\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-E, \\: \\overline{x}+E)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean, and\n- $E$ is the margin of error.\n\nIn our example, the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is also normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\left(\\mu,\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nBy transforming $\\overline{X}$ in the manner described earlier, we have that the random variable $T$, defined as\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nWe wish to find a $t$-interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $T$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/63/63973d7f59a993d42b7b8145f3354e9e7d7dba3c0393c756b6a95754eb4ead2e.png)\n\nSince we wish to find a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we define our parameter $\\alpha$ as\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nis precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as $\\pm t_{n-1, \\alpha/2}$:\n\n![](images/b5/b557afdbd649aa159addd9fe01021a7c3561f94b0168fd874876980d8bb7c3f4.png)\n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{8,0.025}) = P(T < -t_{8,0.025}) = 0.025\n$$\n\nUsing a table of values for the $t$-distribution, we find that\n\n$$\nt_{8,0.025} \\approx 2.306\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(T > 2.306) = P(T < -2.306) = 0.025\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\nP(-2.306 < T < 2.306) = 0.95\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is a $95\\%$ probability that our random variable $T$ will lie in the interval $(-2.306, 2.306)$.\n\nSimilar to the case where $\\sigma^2$ was known, we find the appropriate confidence interval by solving the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the population mean $\\mu$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2.306 & < T < 2.306 \\\\ -2.306 & < (x^(―) - μ)/(s/\\sqrt{n}) < 2.306 \\\\ -2.306 \\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & < x^(―) - μ < 2.306 \\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ -x^(―) - 2.306 \\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & < - μ < - x^(―) + 2.306 \\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ x^(―) + 2.306 \\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & > μ > x^(―) - 2.306 \\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ x^(―) - 2.306 \\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & < μ < x^(―) + 2.306 \\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x}-2.306 \\cdot\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x}+2.306 \\cdot\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n$$\nn=9, \\qquad \\overline{x}=30, \\qquad s=3.3\n$$\n\nwe obtain that our $95\\%$ confidence interval for $\\mu$ is\n\n$$\n\\big(30- 2.537, \\: 30 + 2.537 \\big)\n$$\n\nDescribing this confidence interval using confidence limits, we have\n\n$$\n30 \\pm 2.537\n$$\n\nLet's now describe the general procedure." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186189395 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=9$ drawn from a normal population. The sample mean is $\\overline{x}=30,$ and the sample standard deviation is $s=3.3.$ \nWe wish to compute a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nRecall that a confidence interval is an interval of the form \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-E, \\: \\overline{x}+E),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean, and\n\n- $E$ is the margin of error.\n\nIn our example, the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is also normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\left(\\mu,\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nBy transforming $\\overline{X}$ in the manner described earlier, we have that the random variable $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{S/\\sqrt n},\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nWe wish to find a $t$-interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $T$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/63/63973d7f59a993d42b7b8145f3354e9e7d7dba3c0393c756b6a95754eb4ead2e.png)\n\n  \n\nSince we wish to find a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we define our parameter $\\alpha$ as \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ is precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as $\\pm t_{n-1, \\alpha/2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/b5/b557afdbd649aa159addd9fe01021a7c3561f94b0168fd874876980d8bb7c3f4.png)\n\n  \n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{8,0.025}) = P(T < -t_{8,0.025}) = 0.025.\n$$\n\n  \n\nUsing a table of values for the $t$-distribution, we find that $t_{8,0.025} \\approx 2.306.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(T > 2.306) = P(T < -2.306) = 0.025.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.306<T<2.306)=0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, there is a $95\\%$ probability that our random variable $T$ will lie in the interval $(-2.306, 2.306).$\n\nSimilar to the case where $\\sigma^2$ was known, we find the appropriate confidence interval by solving the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the population mean $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2.306 & <T<2.306 \\\\ -2.306 & <\\frac{\\overset{―}{x}-\\mu }{s/\\sqrt{n}}<2.306 \\\\ -2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & <\\overset{―}{x}-\\mu <2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ -\\overset{―}{x}-2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & <-\\mu <-\\overset{―}{x}+2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{x}+2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & >\\mu >\\overset{―}{x}-2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{x}-2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}} & <\\mu <\\overset{―}{x}+2.306\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x}-2.306 \\cdot\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x}+2.306 \\cdot\\dfrac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}  \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n$$\nn=9, \\qquad\n\\overline{x}=30, \\qquad\ns=3.3,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that our $95\\%$ confidence interval for $\\mu$ is \n\n$$\n\\big( 30- 2.537, \\: 30 + 2.537 \\big).\n$$\n\n \n\nDescribing this confidence interval using confidence limits, we have\n\n$$\n30 \\pm 2.537.\n$$\n\nLet's now describe the general procedure." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186189500 1064 "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n![](images/2c/2c717541104571d6dce45dc88e30c4ff2dd42471d2348fe05b7d91ce395adae4.svg)\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\nX_i \\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p).\n$$\n\nLet's assume the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size. Under this condition, we've seen that $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n$ can be considered to be (approximately) independent and identically distributed, which means that\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p),\n$$\n\nwhere $B(n,p)$ denotes a binomial distribution, and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\nRemember that $X$ represents the number of sample members with the characteristic.\n\nNow, let's assume that $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ so we can apply the normal approximation of the binomial distribution (i.e., the central limit theorem). Then,\n\n$$\nX  \\approx N\\big(np,np(1-p)\\big).\n$$\n\nFurthermore, we've also seen that we can construct an estimator $\\widehat{\\,p}$ as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} =  \\dfrac{X}{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nSince $X$ is approximately normally distributed, $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is also normally distributed. Therefore, the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\approx N\\bigg(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\bigg).\n$$\n\nFinally, since we usually don't know $p,$ we can use a particular estimate to approximate $\\textrm{E}[{\\widehat{\\,p}}]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}].$\n\n**Watch out!** In this lesson, $N$ denotes the size of a population, while $N(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ denotes the normal distribution." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186189500 1064 "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{1, [math]i[/math]th member of the sample has the characteristic; 0, otherwise\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\nX_i \\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p)\n$$\n\nLet's assume the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size. Under this condition, we've seen that $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_n$ can be considered to be (approximately) independent and identically distributed, which means that\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $B(n,p)$ denotes a binomial distribution, and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nRemember that $X$ represents the number of sample members with the characteristic.\n\nNow, let's assume that $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ so we can apply the normal approximation of the binomial distribution (i.e., the central limit theorem). Then,\n\n$$\nX \\approx N\\big(np,np(1-p)\\big)\n$$\n\nFurthermore, we've also seen that we can construct an estimator $\\widehat{\\,p}$ as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = \\dfrac{X}{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\n$$\n\nSince $X$ is approximately normally distributed, $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is also normally distributed. Therefore, the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}\\approx N\\bigg(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\\bigg)\n$$\n\nFinally, since we usually don't know $p$, we can use a particular estimate to approximate $\\textrm{E}[{\\widehat{\\,p}}]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]$.\n\n**Watch out!** In this lesson, $N$ denotes the size of a population, while $N(\\cdot,\\cdot)$ denotes the normal distribution." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186189501 1064 "Let's summarize everything we know about the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$:\n\n> *Suppose we have a population of size $N$ in which a proportion $p$ has a particular characteristic. Consider a sample of size $n$ drawn from the population, where*\n> - $n \\leq 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is smaller than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n> - $np > 5$, *and*\n> - $n(1-p) > 5$.\n> *Then, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as the normally distributed random variable $\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left(p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right)$.*\n\nThe flow chart below summarizes the steps that led us to this model.\n\n![](images/6b/6b9aa832ab1df23e40fe48e52c366d6e5d32a037376b549d6ecc880d2f3832c1.png)\n\nFinally, note that the conditions $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ have a particular interpretation in this context:\n\n- $np > 5$ means the expected number of sample members with the characteristic is greater than $5$,\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ means the expected number of sample members without the characteristic is greater than $5$." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186189501 1064 "Let's summarize everything we know about the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n*Suppose we have a population of size $N$ in which a proportion $p$ has a particular characteristic. Consider a sample of size $n$ drawn from the population, where*\n\n- $n \\leq 5\\%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is smaller than (or equal to) $5\\%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np > 5,$ *and*\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5$.\n\n*Then, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ can be approximated as the normally distributed random variable \n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\sim N\\left( p, \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n} \\right).\n$$\n\n*\n\nThe flow chart below summarizes the steps that led us to this model.\n\n![](images/6b/6b9aa832ab1df23e40fe48e52c366d6e5d32a037376b549d6ecc880d2f3832c1.png)\n\nFinally, note that the conditions $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ have a particular interpretation in this context:\n\n- $np > 5$ means the expected number of sample members with the characteristic is greater than $5,$\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5$ means the expected number of sample members without the characteristic is greater than $5.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186228371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228372 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186228371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228373 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186228371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228374 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186228371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228375 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186228371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228376 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186228371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501500 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186228371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501506 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186228371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501529 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186228371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501913 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186228371 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501939 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186228371 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501529 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186228561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228562 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186228561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228563 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186228561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228564 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186228561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228565 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186228561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186228566 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186228561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501754 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186228561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501910 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186228561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501932 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186228561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501933 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186228561 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501956 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186228561 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501910 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186229181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502082 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186229181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502104 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186229181 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502108 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186229182 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502074 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186229182 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502148 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186229183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502072 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186229183 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502142 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186229261 1050 1500.0 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186229693 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228339 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186229698 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186229698 1071 "A storage tank initially holds $600L$ of liquid containing $5kg$ of dissolved stabilizer.\nA feed solution containing $0.04kg/L$ of the stabilizer is pumped into the tank at a rate of $15L/min$, while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $10L/min$.\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186229698 1071 "A storage tank initially holds $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved stabilizer.\nA feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the stabilizer is pumped into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186229698 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of stabilizer in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which stabilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which stabilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which stabilizer enters the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the stabilizer enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *stabilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which stabilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which stabilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $15\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $10\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n600+15t-10t=(600+5t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of stabilizer in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $10\\,\\text{L/min},$ *stabilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(10\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(10\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{10Q}{600+5t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{120+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{2Q}{120+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{120+t}Q=0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(120+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|120+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(120+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(120+t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(120+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(120+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{120+t}Q) & =(120+t)^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\ (120+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(120+t)Q & =0.6(120+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(120+t)^{2}) & =0.6(120+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(120+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 0.6(120+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(120+t)^{2} & =0.6\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(120+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(120+t)+\\frac{C}{(120+t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $5\\,\\text{kg}$ of stabilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=5,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =5 \\\\ 0.2(120+0)+\\frac{C}{(120+0)^{2}} & =5 \\\\ 24+\\frac{C}{{120}^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{120}^{2}} & =-19 \\\\ C & =-19(120)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(120+t)-\\frac{19(120)^{2}}{(120+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.2(120+t)-19{(\\frac{120}{120+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of stabilizer in the tank $10$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(10) & =0.2(120+10)-19{(\\frac{120}{120+10})}^{2} \\\\  & =26-19{(\\frac{12}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈9.81\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186229724 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186229724 1071 "A wastewater basin initially holds $750L$ of water containing $6kg$ of dissolved pollutant.\nA solution containing $0.03kg/L$ of dissolved pollutant is pumped into the basin at a rate of $30L/min$, while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$. Which expression gives the amount of pollutant $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the basin $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 false] [17592186229724 1071 "A wastewater basin initially holds $750\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved pollutant.\nA solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved pollutant is pumped into the basin at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of pollutant $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the basin $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186229724 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of pollutant in the basin at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which pollutant enters the basin minus the rate at which pollutant leaves the basin.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the basin.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.03\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the pollutant enters the basin at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *pollutant* enters the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.03\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the basin.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the basin remains $750\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of pollutant in the basin, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $30\\,\\text{L/min},$ *pollutant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(30\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(30\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{30Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{25}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.9-\\frac{Q}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}Q=0.9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/25},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{25}\\alpha =0.9\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =22.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=22.5.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/25}+22.5.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved pollutant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/25}+22.5 & =6 \\\\ A & =-16.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=22.5-16.5{e}^{-t/25}.\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186230205 1064 "In the previous lesson, we focused on dilution problems in which the incoming solution contained none of the substance being modeled. In those cases, the rate at which the substance entered the container was zero.\n\nIn many practical situations, however, the incoming solution *does* contain some amount of the substance. When this happens,\n\n- the rate of entry is no longer zero, and\n\n- the resulting differential equation takes