86045417 true] [17592186502068 1039 "Solving Problems Involving Multiplying Negative Fractions" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502068 1041 #edb/ref 17592186502105 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502068 1042 #uuid "aec0cd94-20e4-5748-b9c1-4de7b1854a8e" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502069 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502092 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502069 1038 #edb/ref 17592186502119 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502069 1039 "Solving Problems Involving Multiplying Negative Fractions and Mixed Numbers" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502069 1041 #edb/ref 17592186502120 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502069 1042 #uuid "f589fffd-f5c4-590e-9ed1-01c20390700f" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502008 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502070 1069 "q-337311" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502070 1071 "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves ${x}^{2}y=\\frac{1}{C},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502070 1072 #uuid "0166ce2f-2829-5697-886e-176606ebec4c" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502070 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by ${x}^{2}y=\\frac{1}{C}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({x}^{2}y) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{1}{C}) \\\\ 2xy+{x}^{2}{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{′} & =-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{2y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502010 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502071 1069 "q-110095" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502071 1071 "The lines $y=-3x+1$ and $kx-2y=12$ are parallel. The value of $k=$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502071 1072 #uuid "0a195cf2-04b2-54a7-a4cb-7f79ce0266f0" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502071 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- The line $y=-3x+1$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $-3.$\n\n- To find the slope of the line $kx-2y=12,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx-2y & =12 \\\\ -2y & =-kx+12 \\\\ 2y & =kx-12 \\\\ y & =\\frac{k}{2}x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\frac{k}{2}.$\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have $\\frac{k}{2}=-3.$ Consequently, $k=-6.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502072 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501990 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502072 1069 "q-336519" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502072 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502072 1071 "An ink reservoir for an industrial printer can hold up to $150\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of ink solution with $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved ink dye. A more concentrated ink solution with concentration $\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502072 1072 #uuid "0b0e00ac-15e0-50ff-896e-2fb3f25eb38a" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502072 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir minus the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of ink dye enters the reservoir at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *ink dye* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which ink dye exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which ink dye leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=60+9t-6t=3(20+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of ink dye in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *ink dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{3(20+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{2Q}{20+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(20+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(20+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(20+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{20+t}\\,Q) & =(20+t)^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (20+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(20+t)Q & =12(20+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2}) & =12(20+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(20+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 12(20+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(20+t)^{2} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(20+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(20+t)+\\frac{C}{(20+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of ink dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 4(20+0)+\\frac{C}{(20+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 80+\\frac{C}{(20)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{20}^{2}} & =-62 \\\\ C & =-62(20)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(20+t)-\\frac{62(20)^{2}}{(20+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =4(20+t)-62{(\\frac{20}{20+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $150\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=150$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =150 \\\\ 60+9{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 60+3{t}_{o} & =150 \\\\ 3{t}_{o} & =90 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =30\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of ink dye in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(30) & =4(20+30)-62{(\\frac{20}{20+30})}^{2} \\\\  & =200-62{(\\frac{20}{50})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈190.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502058 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502073 1069 "q-51526" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502073 1071 "What is $\\frac{10g+30}{8g+24}$ reduced to its lowest terms?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502073 1072 #uuid "0dc91759-1708-5aa4-a78f-8164b2208179" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502073 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10g+30}{8g+24} & =\\frac{5(2g+6)}{4(2g+6)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cancel{(2g+6)}}{4\\cancel{(2g+6)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501985 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502074 1069 "q-336746" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502074 1071 "A rinse tank initially holds $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved dye.\nA solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved dye is pumped into the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$ If the amount of dye $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n\n$$\nQ(t)=\\alpha \\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+\\beta {(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants, what is the value of $\\alpha -\\beta ?$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502074 1072 #uuid "11c8e0ff-5f12-56c5-bbf7-d578f71b8d7f" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502074 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $0.025\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.025\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $240\\,\\text{L}$ of water; a solution enters at $16\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $24\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n240+16t-24t=(240-8t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $24\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(24\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24Q}{240-8t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{3Q}{30-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(30-t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30-t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30-t)^{-3}} \\\\  & =(30-t)^{-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30-t)^{-3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{30-t}Q) & =(30-t)^{-3}\\cdot 0.4 \\\\ (30-t)^{-3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(30-t)^{-4}Q & =0.4(30-t)^{-3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3}) & =0.4(30-t)^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30-t)^{-3})\\,dt & =\\int 0.4(30-t)^{-3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30-t)^{-3} & =0.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(30-t)^{-2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+C(30-t)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $9\\,\\text{kg}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=9,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =9 \\\\ C(30-0)^{3}+0.2(30-0) & =9 \\\\ C(30)^{3}+6 & =9 \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(30-t)+\\frac{3}{{30}^{3}}(30-t)^{3} \\\\  & =0.2\\cdot 30\\,(\\frac{30-t}{30})+3{(\\frac{30-t}{30})}^{3} \\\\  & =6\\,(1-\\frac{t}{30})+3{(1-\\frac{t}{30})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have $\\alpha =6,\\beta =3,$ and\n\n$$\n\\alpha -\\beta =6-3=3.\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502075 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502009 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502075 1069 "q-147071" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502075 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502075 1071 "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=99$ from a population where $45%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $50%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ | $\\,0.8238\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ |" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502075 1072 #uuid "11e7255b-c3e0-5351-a566-b77404e961ee" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502075 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=99,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.45.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=99(0.45)=44.55>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=99(1-0.45)=54.45>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=99$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{99}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,0.0025).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.5-0.45}{\\sqrt{0.0025}}) \\\\  & =P(Z<1.00) \\\\  & =Φ(1.00).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.00) & =0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5) & =Φ(0.8413) \\\\  & =0.8413.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502076 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502049 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502076 1069 "q-130553" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502076 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502076 1071 "![](images/0f/0fa5339a6017befb3ac178aa474b6455a5392f79738d2faa3f8861a987c5488b.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Calculate $f(-3)+f(3).$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502076 1072 #uuid "13822b6c-654c-513f-8912-18bafd642a51" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502076 1074 "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=-3,$ the function is undefined.\n\n- When $x=3,$ we have $y=-2.$ So, $f(3)=-2.$\n\nTherefore, since $f(-3)$ is undefined, the sum $f(-3)+f(3)$ is undefined too." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502077 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502042 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502077 1069 "q-193579" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502077 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502077 1071 "Daniel reads $2\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ chapters of a book every hour. How many chapters does he read in $3$ hours?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502077 1072 #uuid "17d541e7-21d6-5b63-bfea-03f360c417cf" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502077 1074 "To find out the number of chapters Daniel reads, we need to multiply $2\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ by $3.$\n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(2\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}.\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{3}{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($3$) and the second fraction's numerator ($3$) have a common factor of $3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{3}{1} & =\\frac{7}{1}\\times \\frac{3}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{1}\\times 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{1} \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Daniel reads $7$ chapters in three hours." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502078 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501984 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502078 1069 "q-146809" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502078 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502078 1071 "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=52$ from a population of size $N=120$ in which $8%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=4$ from a population of size $N=200$ in which $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=20$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502078 1072 #uuid "19ebeffd-a45a-5a50-925e-63646f4bc31a" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502078 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =52, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 120 \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & <52.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =4 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 200 \\\\  & =10\\,✓ \\\\ np & =4(0.7) \\\\  & =2.8 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $np≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =20 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 500 \\\\  & =25\\,✓ \\\\ np & =20(0.70) \\\\  & =14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =20(1-0.70) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502079 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501994 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502079 1069 "q-174396" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502079 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502079 1071 "$$\n{1}_{A}(r)=0,\\,{1}_{B}(r)=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r)=0$\n\nIII. $r\\in A\\cap B$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502079 1072 #uuid "221288b9-d358-51b3-a7a7-1f68c1f5fbe9" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502079 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(r),{1}_{B}(r)\\} \\\\  & =min\\{0,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(r)=0,$ we conclude that $r$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502080 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501991 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502080 1069 "q-60612" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502080 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502080 1071 "A can of bitter lemon drink contains $1\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar. If Phil drank $1$ whole can and $\\frac{3}{5}$ of a second can, how many grams of sugar did he drink?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502080 1072 #uuid "222813e8-d21a-59ba-b697-7bcb16bc7844" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502080 1074 "Notice that Phil drank a total of $1+\\frac{3}{5}=1\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ cans of the beverage. So, to find out how many grams of sugar he drank, we need to calculate $1\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\times 1\\,\\frac{3}{5}.$    \n\nFirst, we write  $1\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{(1\\times 3)+2}{3}=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{(1\\times 5)+3}{5}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($5$) and the second fraction's denominator ($5$) have a common factor of $5.$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}\\times \\frac{8}{5} & =\\frac{5}{5}\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =1\\times \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}=2\\,\\text{R}\\,2=2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, Phil drank $2\\,\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{g}$ of sugar." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502081 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502043 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502081 1069 "q-68493" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502081 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502081 1071 "${(\\frac{2}{3})}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{9}=$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502081 1072 #uuid "22282018-2ca4-523a-8e4c-90a2c94b5770" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502081 1074 "First, we rewrite the first factor using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{9}=\\frac{{2}^{7}}{{3}^{7}}\\cdot {3}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the fractional part of our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{2}^{7}}{{3}^{7}}\\cdot {3}^{9}={2}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{9}}{{3}^{7}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{2}^{7}\\cdot \\frac{{3}^{9}}{{3}^{7}}={2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{9-7}={2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{2}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502082 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502048 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502082 1069 "q-336752" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502082 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502082 1071 "A mixing tank initially holds $600\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleanser.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved cleanser is pumped into the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of cleanser $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502082 1072 #uuid "23f15773-7029-5dc4-9ea6-d17c6a8daaa9" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502082 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of cleanser in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which cleanser enters the tank minus the rate at which cleanser leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleanser enters the tank at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *cleanser* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $600\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of cleanser in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *cleanser* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{600}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{40}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{40}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{40}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{40}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=24.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/40}+24.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $10\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleanser. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/40}+24 & =10 \\\\ A & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=24-14{e}^{-t/40}.\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502083 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502056 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502083 1069 "q-215722" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502083 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502083 1071 "The lines $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4$ and $y=mx+2$ are parallel.  What is the value of $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502083 1072 #uuid "26dd17ae-021a-54b3-9443-27a8619f8c0b" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502083 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+4$ is $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\n- The slope of the line $y=mx+2$ is $m.$\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502084 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502041 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502084 1069 "q-130555" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502084 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502084 1071 "![](images/3c/3c827895f55c7615d8c74b4cc2b25dabaa674108a5889ca78656d812b3a09216.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Calculate $f(-3)+f(2).$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502084 1072 #uuid "26e491f3-a709-5e27-8211-3cc9fc3f4884" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502084 1074 "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=-3,$ we have $y=-2.$ So, $f(-3)=-2.$\n\n- When $x=2,$ the function is undefined.\n\nTherefore, since $f(2)$ is undefined, the sum $f(-3)+f(2)$ is undefined too." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502085 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502026 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502085 1069 "q-147504" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502085 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502085 1071 "It is known that $35%$ of the people arriving at a clothing store buy something. If the manager randomly samples $65$ customers, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $13$ but fewer than $26$ of them bought something. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502085 1072 #uuid "2735f324-7321-5142-98fc-d20d8884d660" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502085 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=65,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.35.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=65(0.35)=22.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=65(1-0.35)=42.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=65$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.35,\\frac{0.35(1-0.35)}{65}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.35,0.0035).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $13$ but fewer than $26$ people bought something, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{65}<\\overset{^}{\\,p} & <\\frac{26}{65} \\\\ 0.2<\\overset{^}{\\,p} & <0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.2\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.2\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.4) & =P(\\frac{0.2-0.35}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}<Z<\\frac{0.4-0.35}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.54<Z<0.85) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.85)-P(Z<-2.54) \\\\  & =Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.2\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.4) & =Φ(0.85)-Φ(-2.54).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502086 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502014 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502086 1069 "q-142574" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502086 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502086 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=16$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $5.4$ and the sample standard deviation is $8,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.947)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $15$ degrees of freedom.*" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502086 1072 #uuid "27ec3d52-24b4-5797-862b-64e2b932e1b5" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502086 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}-{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\\le \\mu \\le \\overset{―}{x}+{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=5.4,\\,n=16,\\,s=8.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.947)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.005}=2.947.\n$$\n\n![](images/bb/bbaa00deb18e38f1b201317615a790764519ad5d2c5b436fe13044a09c71beac.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5.4 & \\pm 2.947\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 5.4 & \\pm 5.894\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502087 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502021 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502087 1069 "q-142518" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502087 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502087 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=81$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $12,$ and the sample standard deviation is $5,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.664)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $80$ degrees of freedom.*" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502087 1072 #uuid "2d606c2e-8088-532b-9f1f-331fecee3381" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502087 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=81\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.  \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=12,\\,n=81,\\,s=5.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.664)=0.05.$ As a result,  \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{80,0.05}=1.664.\n$$\n\n![](images/c1/c1bf3a9f49bb4b4fb24d4fb39bdf0b02743efd1c280875332e5f3bbc1539d953.png)\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & \\pm 1.664\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\ 12 & \\pm 0.924\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502088 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502007 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502088 1069 "q-110102" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502088 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502088 1071 "For what value of $k$ are the lines $2x-4y=8$ and $kx+2y=12$ parallel?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502088 1072 #uuid "2f90abbd-1fee-558b-aa1d-be0b1bb7d5ca" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502088 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\n- To find the slope of the line $2x-4y=8,$ we convert to slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y & =8 \\\\ -4y & =-2x+8 \\\\ 4y & =2x-8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- To find the slope of the line $kx+2y=12,$ we convert to slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kx+2y & =12 \\\\ 2y & =-kx+12 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{k}{2}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $-\\frac{k}{2}.$\n\nSince the slopes of the two lines must be equal, we must have $-\\frac{k}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.$ We solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{k}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{k}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ k & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502089 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502033 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502089 1069 "q-337573" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502089 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502089 1071 "Find the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C$ and $K$ are parameters." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502089 1072 #uuid "33804b3c-aea9-58d0-9654-8a84f729c14e" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502089 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x}{y}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{y-x{y}^{′}}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ y-x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{x})}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =-x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int (-x)\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =-\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a3/a3854e6c47f28ce601ef2395379bea788e954c4e4057331772b5840cd7073237.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502090 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502050 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502090 1069 "q-174358" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502090 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502090 1071 "![](images/bb/bbd8b82211b2b3a19935aae010e15083ce3553984473444ae6f7a720b682d7f5.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the subset $A$ of $U=\\{p,q,r,s,t\\}$ whose indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ is shown above?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502090 1072 #uuid "33d9e7d3-a57a-5882-990d-c4b03eec3609" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502090 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the diagram above, only the elements $q$ and $t$ are mapped to $1.$ Therefore, the indicator function in the diagram above corresponds to the set $A=\\{q,t\\}.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502091 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501999 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502091 1069 "q-212001" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502091 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502091 1071 "A farmer has five $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$-acre fields. He planted wheat in $3$ fields and in $\\frac{1}{2}$ of another field. How many acres of wheat did the farmer plant?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502091 1072 #uuid "35b950d1-e08b-5f6b-af7f-1977577a5d81" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502091 1074 "Notice that the farmer planted wheat in $3+\\frac{1}{2}=3\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ fields. So, to find out the total acres, we need to calculate $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times 3\\,\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}=\\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe also write $3\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(3\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{4}\\times \\frac{7}{2}=\\frac{9\\times 7}{4\\times 2}=\\frac{63}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{63}{8}=7\\,\\text{R}\\,7=7\\,\\frac{7}{8}\n$$\n\nSo, the farmer planted $7\\,\\frac{7}{8}$ acres of wheat." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501981 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502092 1069 "q-357243" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502092 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502092 1071 "The temperature inside a freezer decreases by $\\frac{3}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute for $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502092 1072 #uuid "361640a2-f197-5749-a9e6-5d3176000185" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502092 1074 "The temperature decreases by $\\frac{3}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $1\\,\\frac{1}{4}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\times (-\\frac{3}{5}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the mixed number as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1\\times 4+1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nSo the expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\\times (-\\frac{3}{5}).\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the product:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{4}\\times (-\\frac{3}{5})=-\\frac{5\\times 3}{4\\times 5}=-\\frac{15}{20}=-\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502093 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502034 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502093 1069 "q-134167" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502093 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502093 1071 "Calculate $\\text{Cov}(x,y)$ for the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{1,\\,4,\\,5,\\,6\\},\\,y=\\{4,\\,5,\\,2,\\,1\\}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502093 1072 #uuid "3623d58e-6308-55c7-b844-a67fd7315f21" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502093 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (1+4+5+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (4+5+2+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(1-4)(4-3)+(4-4)(5-3)+(5-4)(2-3)+(6-4)(1-3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-3)(1)+(0)(2)+(1)(-1)+(2)(-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-3+0-1-4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502059 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502094 1069 "q-337317" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502094 1071 "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C),$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502094 1072 #uuid "3933c79b-01f9-56d3-9429-e1ebc3d9a1ea" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502094 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C).$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(C)) \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)})}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x>0,$ $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\ne 0,$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502040 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502095 1069 "q-143026" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502095 1071 "A thread manufacturer tests the tensile strength of a sample containing $9$ pieces of a particular type of thread.\nThe mean tensile strength of the sample is $7.8\\,\\text{lb}$ with a standard deviation of $0.06\\,\\text{lb}.$\nAssuming that tensile strengths are normally distributed, find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean tensile strength  $\\mu $ of this thread. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $\\,1.860\\,$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502095 1072 #uuid "3c887832-1efe-5629-bd9f-7e1600f20497" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502095 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=7.8\\,\\text{lb},\\,n=9,\\,s=0.06\\,\\text{lb}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{8,0.005}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{8,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=9-1=8$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/11/1199336fffafda6d04b4e9562aec6b1bbc1726005a54180c8e8ed4935981ddd1.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{8,0.005}=3.355:$  \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $\\,1.860\\,$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $3.355$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |\n\n   \n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.8 & \\pm 3.355\\cdot \\frac{0.06}{\\sqrt{9}}\\,\\text{lb} \\\\ 7.8 & \\pm 0.067\\,\\text{lb}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502031 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502096 1069 "q-161886" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502096 1071 "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(3,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-v \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502096 1072 #uuid "3d5c488c-135d-5c5b-b6fb-380729a9e440" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502096 1074 "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot {{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{⏟}}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2} & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}-1 \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(3,1)} & =(3{u}^{2}-1)\\,{|}_{u=3\\,v=1} \\\\  & =3(3)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|26|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =26\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502020 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502097 1069 "q-52391" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502097 1071 "$3\\sqrt{x}-2\\sqrt{x}=$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502097 1072 #uuid "3efd4daf-a1cf-5957-b4aa-c8ff94e19b4d" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502097 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{x}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{x}-2\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ (3-2)\\sqrt{x} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501997 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502098 1069 "q-134506" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502098 1071 "$2\\sqrt{p}-\\sqrt{16p}+\\sqrt[3]{p}+\\sqrt[3]{{p}^{4}}=$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502098 1072 #uuid "40cade47-943f-50c4-8cf4-c4d5f9b88bc8" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502098 1074 "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{p}-\\sqrt{16p}+\\sqrt[3]{p}+\\sqrt[3]{{p}^{4}} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{p}-\\sqrt{16\\cdot p}+\\sqrt[3]{p}+\\sqrt[3]{{p}^{3}\\cdot p} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{p}-\\sqrt{16}\\cdot \\sqrt{p}+\\sqrt[3]{p}+\\sqrt[3]{{p}^{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{p} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{p}-4\\sqrt{p}+\\sqrt[3]{p}+p\\sqrt[3]{p} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{p}-4\\sqrt{p}+\\sqrt[3]{p}+p\\sqrt[3]{p} & = \\\\ (2-4)\\sqrt{p}+(1+p)\\sqrt[3]{p} & = \\\\ -2\\sqrt{p}+(1+p)\\sqrt[3]{p} & = \\\\ (p+1)\\sqrt[3]{p}-2\\sqrt{p}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot combine the $\\sqrt[3]{p}$ and $\\sqrt{p}$ terms." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502023 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502099 1069 "q-193555" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502099 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502099 1071 "Mario fills three bottles with water. If the capacity of every bottle is $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ liters, how much water did he use to fill the bottles?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502099 1072 #uuid "42bf2d9b-a245-5919-95ec-686175cfea53" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502099 1074 "To determine the total volume of water used to fill the bottles, we need to multiply $3$ by $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nFirst, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe also write $3$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3=\\frac{3}{1}.\n$$\n\nSo now, we need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's numerator ($3$) and the second fraction's denominator ($3$) have a common factor of $3.$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by swapping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3} & =\\frac{3}{3}\\times \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =1\\times \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mario used $4$ liters of water to fill the bottles." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502100 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502017 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502100 1069 "q-336685" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502100 1071 "A tank initially holds $420\\,\\text{gal}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $14\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt.\nFresh water is pumped into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of salt in the tank $20$ minutes after the fresh water pumping begins." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502100 1072 #uuid "4779b0e3-7c68-54eb-98ad-ac554dad498b" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502100 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of salt in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nNote that fresh water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt) enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $420\\,\\text{gal}$ of brine; water enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and drains out at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n420+12t-12t=420\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{420}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{35}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{Q}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{1}{35}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{1}{35} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{35})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-\\frac{1}{35}\\int 1\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-\\frac{t}{35}+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{-\\frac{t}{35}+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =A{e}^{-t/35},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $14\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=14,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =14 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/35} & =14 \\\\ A & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=14{e}^{-t/35}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of salt in the tank $20$ minutes after the fresh water is first pumped in is\n\n$$\nQ(20)=14{e}^{-20/35}≈7.91\\,\\text{lb},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502101 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501987 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502101 1069 "q-34206" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502101 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502101 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{192z}-\\sqrt{147z}.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502101 1072 #uuid "48442579-8421-52f2-adbc-f2ba91a631dd" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502101 1074 "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{192z}-\\sqrt{147z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{64\\cdot 3z}-\\sqrt{49\\cdot 3z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{64}\\cdot \\sqrt{3z}-\\sqrt{49}\\cdot \\sqrt{3z} & = \\\\ 8\\sqrt{3z}-7\\sqrt{3z} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{3z}-7\\sqrt{3z} & = \\\\ (8-7)\\sqrt{3z} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{3z} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502102 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502065 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502102 1069 "q-142506" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502102 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502102 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=225$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $98,$ and the sample standard deviation is $30,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.598)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $224$ degrees of freedom.*" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502102 1072 #uuid "4ae17796-447a-5d04-9973-717e1d847688" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502102 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=225\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=98,\\,n=225,\\,s=30.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.598)=0.005.$ As a result,\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{224,0.005}=2.598.\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2b711e6fe35f64d886472fa38c5a65afd7473b344949d7effd49405946f3cee5.png)\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}98 & \\pm 2.598\\cdot \\frac{30}{\\sqrt{225}} \\\\ 98 & \\pm 5.196\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502103 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501976 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502103 1069 "q-142701" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502103 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502103 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=16$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $45$ and the sample standard deviation is $9,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.947)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $15$ degrees of freedom.*" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502103 1072 #uuid "4c5ba2c5-2aab-5530-84f9-62121bb3609f" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502103 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=45,\\,n=16,\\,s=9.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.947)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.005}=2.947.\n$$\n\n![](images/bb/bbaa00deb18e38f1b201317615a790764519ad5d2c5b436fe13044a09c71beac.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 45\\pm 2.947\\cdot \\frac{9}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & 45\\pm 6.631\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502104 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501996 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502104 1069 "q-336736" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502104 1071 "A mixing tank initially holds $400\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleaning agent.\nA feed solution containing $0.08\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleaning agent is pumped into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of cleaning agent in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502104 1072 #uuid "554f59f6-c41f-55e2-8f84-8c2aab7670ec" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502104 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of cleaning agent in the tank at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which cleaning agents enter the tank minus the rate at which cleaning agents leave the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which cleaning agents enter the tank.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.08\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the cleaning agent enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *cleaning agent* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.08\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.08\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which cleaning agents exit the tank.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the tank remains $400\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of cleaning agent in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *cleaning agent* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{400}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=1.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =1.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =32.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=32.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+32.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $6\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved cleaning agent. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=6,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =6 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+32 & =6 \\\\ A & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=32-26{e}^{-t/20}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of cleaning agent in the tank $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\nQ(5)=32-26{e}^{-5/20}≈11.75\\,\\text{kg},\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502105 1069 "e-22714" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502105 1071 "The temperature of a liquid decreases by $\\dfrac{4}{5}\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ each minute for $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502105 1072 #uuid "5f3cfae7-b7d6-56b2-a245-6ec44b76e462" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502105 1074 "The temperature decreases by $\\dfrac{4}{5}\\,^\\circ\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\dfrac{4}{5}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2} \\times \\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{2} \\times \\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)\n= -\\dfrac{3 \\times 4}{2 \\times 5}\n= -\\dfrac{12}{10}\n= -\\dfrac{6}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-\\dfrac{6}{5}\\,^\\circ\\text{C}.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502013 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502106 1069 "q-52367" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502106 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{2x-6}{3-x}?$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502106 1072 #uuid "624c7b11-aded-592e-be21-f98c6f89890c" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502106 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-6}{3-x} & =\\frac{2(x-3)}{-(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (x-3)}{(-1)\\cdot (x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{(x-3)}}{(-1)\\cdot \\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502037 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502107 1069 "q-357179" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502107 1071 "The temperature in a city decreases by $1.2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each hour for $5$ hours. What is the total change in temperature after $5$ hours?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502107 1072 #uuid "62ed462d-094a-533c-8c38-e7a5c2d0177d" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502107 1074 "The temperature decreases by $1.2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each hour.\n\nSince the change is a decrease and occurs for $5$ hours, the total change can be written as repeated subtraction of $1.2:$\n\n$$\n{{-\\,1.2-1.2-1.2-1.2-1.2}_{⏟}}_{5\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nRepeated subtraction can also be written using multiplication:\n\n$$\n5\\times (-1.2)\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n5\\times (-1.2)=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature after $5$ hours is $-6{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502108 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502054 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502108 1069 "q-339383" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502108 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502108 1071 "A pharmaceutical mixer initially holds $300\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $1\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved medication.\nA solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved medication is pumped into the mixer at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which expression gives the amount of medication $Q(t),$ in kilograms, in the mixer $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502108 1072 #uuid "63623e2d-9a1a-53b1-a242-66f71be1ef8a" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502108 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of medication in the mixer at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which medication enters the mixer minus the rate at which medication leaves the mixer.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which additives enter the mixer.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.04\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the medication enters the mixer at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *medication* enters the mixer is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.04\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which additives exit the mixer.\n\nNext, note that the rate of flow in equals the rate of flow out, so the volume in the mixer remains $300\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, since $Q$ is the amount of medication in the mixer, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $15\\,\\text{L/min},$ *medication* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(15\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15Q}{300}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{20}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.6-\\frac{Q}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}Q=0.6.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${Q}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{Q}_{c}}{dt}+\\frac{1}{20}{Q}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{Q}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{Q}_{p}(t)=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${Q}_{p}=\\alpha $ and $\\frac{d{Q}_{p}}{dt}=(\\alpha )^{′}=0$ into the inhomogeneous equation:\n\n$$\n0+\\frac{1}{20}\\alpha =0.6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =12.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is ${Q}_{p}(t)=12.$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nQ(t)={Q}_{c}(t)+{Q}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t/20}+12.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the mixer initially contains $1\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved medication. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=1,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =1 \\\\ A{e}^{-0/20}+12 & =1 \\\\ A & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\nQ(t)=12-11{e}^{-t/20}.\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501982 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502109 1069 "q-28288" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502109 1071 "A line $l$ is parallel to $4x-2y=6.$ A possible equation for $l$ is" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502109 1072 #uuid "63caf14b-4a45-5fc8-9446-996c19cf7a2b" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502109 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $4x-2y=6,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2y & =6 \\\\ -2y & =-4x+6 \\\\ y & =2x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $2.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $2$ is $y=2x+6.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502001 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502110 1069 "q-174377" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502110 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502110 1071 "Which of the following is the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of the subset $A=\\{3,5\\}$ of $X=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502110 1072 #uuid "658d443c-02c1-52f6-a2b6-cd97f500f835" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502110 1074 "An indicator function maps elements from a set to the values $0$ or $1,$ depending on whether the element belongs to a specified subset. The indicator function is designed to \"indicate\" whether an element is a member of a subset.\n\nMore specifically, consider a universal set $X.$ Then, the indicator function of a set $A⊆X$ is the function\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }x\\in A, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }x\\notin A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe indicator function of the set $\\{3,5\\}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed92f46f57aefc4d9c7445f044f1b35adb89950c16f331aff35c02445b607c95.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the elements $3$ and $5$ (the elements of our subset) are mapped to $1,$ and all other elements are mapped to $0.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502002 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502111 1069 "q-142505" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502111 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=121$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $12.5,$ and the sample standard deviation is $1.65,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.617)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $120$ degrees of freedom.*" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502111 1072 #uuid "6b03a1f2-3bac-59ff-a005-6c994dad1ca7" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502111 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=121\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=12.5,\\,n=121,\\,s=1.65.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.617)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{120,0.005}=2.617.\n$$\n\n![](images/18/18456a4a686aae65f078015f1669cad8e1316b66166567f22df35345ebd87fa4.png)\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12.5 & \\pm 2.617\\cdot \\frac{1.65}{\\sqrt{121}} \\\\ 12.5 & \\pm 0.393\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502039 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502112 1069 "q-147212" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502112 1071 "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=125$ from a population where $75%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $65%$ but less than $72%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2266\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2236\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2206\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2578\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2546\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2514\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2912\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2877\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2843\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0052\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0051\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0049\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0069\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0068\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0066\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0091\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0089\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0087\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502112 1072 #uuid "6c90ea0b-854a-5f39-a73d-92e856698eb8" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502112 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.75.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=125(0.75)=93.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=125(1-0.75)=31.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=125$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.75,\\frac{0.75(1-0.75)}{125}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.75,0.0015).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.65<\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.72).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.65<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.72) & =P(\\frac{0.65-0.75}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}<Z\\le \\frac{0.72-0.75}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.58<Z\\le -0.77) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.77)-P(Z\\le -2.58) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.77)-Φ(-2.58)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.77) & =0.2206 \\\\ Φ(-2.58) & =0.0049.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.65<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.72) & =Φ(-0.77)-Φ(-2.58) \\\\  & =0.2206-0.0049 \\\\  & =0.2157.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502022 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502113 1069 "q-68952" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502113 1071 "${2}^{3}\\cdot {(3\\cdot 2)}^{4}=$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502113 1072 #uuid "6ef8e162-2bcb-5b53-83cc-147de68407b1" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502113 1074 "First, we rewrite the second factor using the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n{2}^{3}\\cdot {(3\\cdot 2)}^{4}={2}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{4}\\cdot {2}^{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule for exponents, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{4}\\cdot {2}^{4} & ={2}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{4}\\cdot {3}^{4} \\\\  & ={2}^{3+4}\\cdot {3}^{4} \\\\  & ={2}^{7}\\cdot {3}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502063 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502114 1069 "q-146819" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502114 1071 "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=200$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $80%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=30$ from a population of size $N=1\\,000$ in which $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502114 1072 #uuid "75b5d8b1-9316-5672-a007-ededd1581f02" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502114 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =200 \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & <200\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.8) \\\\  & =32 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.8) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,000 \\\\  & =50\\,✓ \\\\ np & =30(0.7) \\\\  & =21 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =30(1-0.7) \\\\  & =9 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502115 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501988 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502115 1069 "q-336622" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502115 1071 "A tank initially holds $280\\,\\text{L}$ of a chemical solution containing $4\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved chemical.\nA chemical solution containing $0.20\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved chemical per liter is poured into the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of dissolved chemical $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the solution begins to be added?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502115 1072 #uuid "787955c7-f519-56e2-a7ff-67abb164ab3b" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502115 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of dissolved chemical in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dissolved chemical enters the tank minus the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a chemical solution containing $0.20\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of dissolved chemical enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dissolved chemical* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.20\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.20\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =3.2\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dissolved chemical exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which dissolved chemical leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $280\\,\\text{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Chemical solution enters the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the tank at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n280+16t-16t=280\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of dissolved chemical in the tank, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ *dissolved chemical* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16Q}{280}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{35}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =3.2-\\frac{2Q}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{35}Q=3.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502055 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502116 1069 "q-142512" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502116 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=256$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $105,$ and the sample standard deviation is $36,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.969)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $255$ degrees of freedom.*" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502116 1072 #uuid "79c0fa2f-3630-597c-948c-a11745cb42f9" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502116 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=256\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=105,\\,n=256,\\,s=36.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.969)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{255,0.025}=1.969.\n$$\n\n![](images/d1/d108130264501813e8bfed332f345d8d005095127c5d9d8e169a1c2ae83b2ec7.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}105 & \\pm 1.969\\cdot \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{256}} \\\\ 105 & \\pm 4.430\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502117 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502012 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502117 1069 "q-147448" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502117 1071 "An amusement park knows that $25%$ of its visitors ride the rollercoaster. The manager of the park conducts a random sample of $125$ visitors. Find a normal approximation for the probability that fewer than $40$ of them rode the rollercoaster. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502117 1072 #uuid "7a5fcfb6-efc6-55da-8a4d-4dde0bab77e9" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502117 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.25.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=125(0.25)=31.25>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=125(1-0.25)=93.75>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=125$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.25,\\frac{0.25(1-0.25)}{125}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.25,0.0015).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $40$ visitors in the sample rode the rollercoaster, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}<\\frac{40}{125}=0.32.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.32).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.32-0.25}{\\sqrt{0.0015}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<1.81) \\\\  & =Φ(1.81).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502118 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502025 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502118 1069 "q-142917" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502118 1071 "A random sample of $100$ students from a particular university yields a mean GPA of $3.1$ with a sample standard deviation of $0.48.$ Find a  $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean GPA $\\mu $ of all students.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $99$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $100$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $101$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.625\\,$ |" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502118 1072 #uuid "7a68a1f9-858f-5d69-bc49-809a211decce" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502118 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=3.1,\\,n=100,\\,s=0.48.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{99,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{99,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=100-1=99$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/1b/1bb4e66b68bbc1fb55a36abafb4f184fb6b25b16314e989a47fe6b3a835cea65.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{99,0.05}=1.660:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $99$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $1.660$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $100$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $101$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.625\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.1 & \\pm 1.66\\cdot \\frac{0.48}{\\sqrt{100}}\\,\\text{grade points} \\\\ 3.1 & \\pm 0.08\\text{ grade points}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502036 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502119 1069 "q-357241" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502119 1071 "The temperature of a chemical solution decreases by $\\frac{4}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute for $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502119 1072 #uuid "7b837a54-b40c-5dbd-9c93-92d2c8b04271" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502119 1074 "The temperature decreases by $\\frac{4}{5}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $3\\,\\frac{3}{4}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the mixed number as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 4+3}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nSo the expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5}).\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the product:\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{4}\\times (-\\frac{4}{5})=-\\frac{15\\times 4}{4\\times 5}=-\\frac{60}{20}=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-3{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502120 1069 "e-22729" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502120 1071 "A submarine descends $1\\,\\dfrac{3}{8}\\,\\text{m}$ each minute from sea level. What is the submarine's elevation after $\\dfrac{8}{3}$ minutes?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502120 1072 #uuid "7be163d0-2103-5467-9bba-a4090855cc8b" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502120 1074 "The submarine descends $1\\,\\dfrac{3}{8}\\,\\text{m}$ each minute.\n\nSince the elevation decreases, the change each minute is $-1\\,\\dfrac{3}{8}.$\n\nThe descent lasts $\\dfrac{8}{3}$ minutes, so the total change in elevation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3} \\times \\left(-1\\,\\dfrac{3}{8}\\right).\n$$\n\nFirst, we write the mixed number as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n-1\\,\\dfrac{3}{8} = -\\dfrac{1\\times8 + 3}{8} = -\\dfrac{11}{8}\n$$\n\nSo the expression becomes\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3} \\times \\left(-\\dfrac{11}{8}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{3} \\times \\left(-\\dfrac{11}{8}\\right)\n= -\\dfrac{8 \\times 11}{3 \\times 8}\n= -\\dfrac{11}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the submarine's elevation after $\\dfrac{8}{3}$ minutes is $-\\dfrac{11}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502046 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502121 1069 "q-60535" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502121 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502121 1071 "A country road is $2\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{mi}$ long. If $\\frac{3}{4}$ of it is paved with asphalt, how long is the paved part?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502121 1072 #uuid "7c7467de-330b-5939-83a9-4571e046a2da" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502121 1074 "To find out the length of the paved part of the road, we need to calculate $2\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{4}.$ \n\nFirst, we write $2\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{(2\\times 2)+1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{5\\times 3}{2\\times 4}=\\frac{15}{8}\n$$\n\nFinally, by converting this fraction to a mixed number, we get\n\n$$\n15÷8=1\\,\\text{R}\\,7=1\\,\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the paved part of the road is $1\\,\\frac{7}{8}\\,\\text{mi}$ long." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502122 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501983 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502122 1069 "q-336551" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502122 1071 "A reactor initially holds $520\\,\\text{L}$ of a chemical solution containing $8\\,\\text{kg}$ of reactant.\nA pure solvent is poured into the reactor at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed solution leaves the reactor at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of reactant $Q,$ in kilograms, in the reactor $t$ minutes after the solvent begins to be added?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502122 1072 #uuid "7ce0dbae-abd1-5d37-8894-76e5b590f727" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502122 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of reactant in the reactor at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which reactant enters the reactor minus the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which reactant enters the reactor.\n\nFirst, note that the incoming solvent (i.e., a solution containing $0\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of reactant) enters the reactor at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *reactant* enters the reactor is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (16\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which reactant exits the reactor.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which reactant leaves the reactor, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reactor at time $t.$\n\n- The reactor initially contains $520\\,\\text{L}$ of solution.\n\n- Solvent enters the reactor at a rate of $16\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $16t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the reactor.\n\n- The completely mixed solution leaves the reactor at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the reactor.\n\nAs a result, the volume of solution in the reactor after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n520+16t-20t=(520-4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of reactant in the reactor, the *concentration* of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *reactant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{520-4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{130-t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{5Q}{130-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{5}{130-t}Q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501995 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502123 1069 "q-146942" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502123 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502123 1071 "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=50$ from a population where $20%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $15%$ and $25%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502123 1072 #uuid "84cf65c5-831d-5253-bb62-b35c4c8f232c" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502123 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=50,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.2.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=50(0.2)=10>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=50(1-0.2)=40>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=50$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.2,\\frac{0.2(1-0.2)}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.2,0.0032).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.15\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.25).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.15\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.25) & =P(\\frac{0.15-0.2}{\\sqrt{0.0032}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.25-0.2}{\\sqrt{0.0032}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.88\\le Z\\le 0.88) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.88)-P(Z\\le -0.88) \\\\  & =Φ(0.88)-Φ(-0.88)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501980 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502124 1069 "q-100757" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502124 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502124 1071 "${(6\\cdot 8)}^{3}\\cdot {6}^{5}=$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502124 1072 #uuid "8bec425d-e327-5350-ad55-2bf5d8347c9a" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502124 1074 "First, we rewrite the second factor using the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n{(6\\cdot 8)}^{3}\\cdot {6}^{5}={6}^{3}\\cdot {8}^{3}\\cdot {6}^{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the product rule for exponents, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{3}\\cdot {8}^{3}\\cdot {6}^{5} & ={6}^{3}\\cdot {6}^{5}\\cdot {8}^{3} \\\\  & ={6}^{3+5}\\cdot {8}^{3} \\\\  & ={6}^{8}\\cdot {8}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501975 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502125 1069 "q-130517" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502125 1071 "![](images/9f/9f744e2729965bffbf281aee4a2bb2ecbdca635035e5fefca8f992d59d2590a1.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is given by the graph above. Calculate $f(1)+f(4).$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502125 1072 #uuid "930ee824-1c66-5c5d-8da9-b30bfa636dd4" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502125 1074 "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=1,$ we have $y=2.$ So, $f(1)=2.$\n\n- When $x=4,$ we have $y=3.$ So, $f(4)=3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1)+f(4)=2+3=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502004 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502126 1069 "q-179508" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502126 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502126 1071 "Written in the form $y=mx+b,$ what is the equation of the line that passes through the points $(6,-2)$ and $(0,2)?$\n\nThe equation of the line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502126 1072 #uuid "9340bec6-b3d6-57e0-89a1-d4e7fb7357f7" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502126 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-(-2)}{0-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+2}{0-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{-6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are always $(0,b).$ We know that the line passes through the point $(0,2),$ so the $y$-intercept is $b=2.$  \n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and the $y$-intercept $b=2$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2.\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502024 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502127 1069 "q-174406" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502127 1071 "$$\n{1}_{A}(2)=0,\\,{1}_{B}(2)=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values of the indicator functions of the sets $A$ and $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2)=1$\n\nII. ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2)=0$\n\nIII. $2\\in A\\cup B$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502127 1072 #uuid "95e868f8-4928-5f0b-9ec7-afed42df8e19" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502127 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(2),{1}_{B}(2)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{0,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(2)=0,$ we conclude that $2$ does not belong to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502052 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502128 1069 "q-44382" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502128 1071 "A line $l$ is parallel to $4x-y=22.$  A possible equation for $l$ is" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502128 1072 #uuid "9815706a-98d4-5baf-aff8-15181269835f" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502128 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $4x-y=22,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-y & =22 \\\\ -y & =-4x+22 \\\\ y & =4x-22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $4.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $4$ is $y=4x+8.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502129 1069 "e-22728" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502129 1071 "Over the course of $6$ hours, the temperature of a liquid decreases by $9.6^\\circ\\text{C}$ in total. Assuming the temperature decreases at a constant rate, what is the change in temperature each hour?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502129 1072 #uuid "9c400da4-eba5-58aa-8ee5-a33269adfa2a" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502129 1074 "Since the temperature decreases by $9.6^\\circ\\text{C}$ over $6$ hours, the total change is $-9.6^\\circ\\text{C}.$\n\nThis change occurs evenly over $6$ hours, so we divide the total change by $6{:}$\n\n$$\n-9.6 \\div 6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the quotient, we get\n\n$$\n-9.6 \\div 6 = -1.6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature changes by $-1.6^\\circ\\text{C}$ each hour." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501992 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502130 1069 "q-336843" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502130 1071 "A $300\\,\\text{gal}$-capacity tank initially holds $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of water containing $45\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Find the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502130 1072 #uuid "9f051584-9c22-538b-be73-a90ad4399f9f" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502130 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of water; clean water enters at $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ and mixed water leaves at $4\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=180+6t-4t=(180+2t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{180+2t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{90+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{90+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{90+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{90+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{90+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(90+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(90+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $45\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=45,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =45 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(90+0)^{2}} & =45 \\\\ A & =45(90)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{45(90)^{2}}{(90+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =45{(\\frac{90}{90+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $300\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=300$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =300 \\\\ 180+2{t}_{o} & =300 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =60\\,\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(60) & =45{(\\frac{90}{90+60})}^{2} \\\\  & =45{(\\frac{90}{150})}^{2} \\\\  & =45{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =16.20\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502064 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502131 1069 "q-146975" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502131 1071 "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=76$ from a population where $24%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $30%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8849\\,$ | $\\,0.8869\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9032\\,$ | $\\,0.9049\\,$ | $\\,0.9066\\,$ | $\\,0.9082\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9192\\,$ | $\\,0.9207\\,$ | $\\,0.9222\\,$ | $\\,0.9236\\,$ |" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502131 1072 #uuid "a0e2a9c0-d660-51ad-8aa9-baeec300d0b3" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502131 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=76,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.24.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=76(0.24)=18.24>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=76(1-0.24)=57.76>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=76$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.24,\\frac{0.24(1-0.24)}{76}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.24,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.3).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.3) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.3-0.24}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.22) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<1.22) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.22).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.22) & =0.8888.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.3) & =1-Φ(1.22) \\\\  & =1-0.8888 \\\\  & =0.1112.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502051 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502132 1069 "q-146838" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502132 1071 "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of $50$ recently caught shrimps, where $55%$ of the shrimps in the sea weigh more than $10\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nII. A sample of $27$ companies, where $82%$ of the companies in the country employ more than $100$ people.\n\nIII. A sample of $100$ songs in a streaming service, given that $92%$ of the songs last less than $3$ minutes.\n\n*Hint: In each case, you may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502132 1072 #uuid "ab0bcefd-4d59-56aa-b8ec-f3d0b1dc4757" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502132 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nWe're given that the population is significantly larger than the sample size in each case. Therefore, we may assume that the first condition is satisfied in each case.\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50(0.55) \\\\  & =27.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50(1-0.55) \\\\  & =22.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =27(0.82) \\\\  & =22.14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =27(1-0.82) \\\\  & =4.86 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100(0.92) \\\\  & =92 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =100(1-0.92) \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501986 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502133 1069 "q-111028" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502133 1071 "${n}^{2}\\sqrt{27n}+\\sqrt{48{n}^{5}}=$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502133 1072 #uuid "ad9cae8a-9ebc-5c61-bc98-540e57a392b2" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502133 1074 "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}\\sqrt{27n}+\\sqrt{48{n}^{5}} & = \\\\ {n}^{2}\\sqrt{9\\cdot 3n}+\\sqrt{16{n}^{4}\\cdot 3n} & = \\\\ {n}^{2}\\sqrt{9}\\cdot \\sqrt{3n}+\\sqrt{16{n}^{4}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3n} & = \\\\ 3{n}^{2}\\sqrt{3n}+4{n}^{2}\\sqrt{3n} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{n}^{2}\\sqrt{3n}+4{n}^{2}\\sqrt{3n} & = \\\\ (3+4){n}^{2}\\sqrt{3n} & = \\\\ 7{n}^{2}\\sqrt{3n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501978 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502134 1069 "q-142571" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502134 1071 "![](images/4c/4c9b1e09734e74c098fd5e91eb3c72079157c3a69b1bb1d039bb46e823e5b7b6.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=25$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $35.5,$ and the sample standard deviation is $3,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.064)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $24$ degrees of freedom,  as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502134 1072 #uuid "aecd358b-06cc-5c20-9edb-c893e2b56662" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502134 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom. \n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=35.5,\\,n=25,\\,s=3.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.064)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{24,0.025}=2.064.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}35.5 & \\pm 2.064\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\ 35.5 & \\pm 1.238\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502011 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502135 1069 "q-142515" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502135 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=400$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $57,$ and the sample standard deviation is $26,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.649)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $399$ degrees of freedom.*" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502135 1072 #uuid "b0647362-6c93-5a80-87bb-0e4349d8ef9c" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502135 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=400\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=57,\\,n=400,\\,s=26.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.649)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{399,0.05}=1.649.\n$$\n\n![](images/7d/7d2cc71801ad3826dddfb670518ad038ccbf11f363569896fe3c3b673e5c58a6.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}57 & \\pm 1.649\\cdot \\frac{26}{\\sqrt{400}} \\\\ 57 & \\pm 2.144\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502045 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502136 1069 "q-357178" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502136 1071 "The temperature inside a freezer drops by $2.5{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ every hour for $4$ hours. What is the total change in temperature after $4$ hours?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502136 1072 #uuid "b43654ae-4e3f-5d53-a4fb-91c5e392ca63" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502136 1074 "The temperature decreases by $2.5{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each hour.\n\nSince the change is a decrease and occurs for $4$ hours, the total change can be written as repeated subtraction of $2.5:$\n\n$$\n{{-\\,2.5-2.5-2.5-2.5}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nRepeated subtraction can also be written using multiplication:\n\n$$\n4\\times (-2.5)\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n4\\times (-2.5)=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature after $4$ hours is $-10{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501977 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502137 1069 "q-44380" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502137 1071 "A line $l$ is parallel to $6x-2y=11.$ A possible equation for $l$ is" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502137 1072 #uuid "b444fd71-00fa-57c3-8df1-8666ae2725d8" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502137 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $6x-2y=11,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-2y & =11 \\\\ \\frac{6}{2}x-\\frac{2}{2}y & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ 3x-y & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ -y & =-3x+\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ y & =3x-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $3.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $3$ is $y=3x-8.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502030 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502138 1069 "q-134508" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502138 1071 "$7-\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{4}}+6t\\sqrt[3]{2t}=$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502138 1072 #uuid "b5988724-f4b1-5a52-8719-b7ac5bee89d1" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502138 1074 "We simplify each of the radical terms using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-\\sqrt[3]{2{t}^{4}}+6t\\sqrt[3]{2t} & = \\\\ 7-\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}\\cdot 2t}+6t\\sqrt[3]{2t} & = \\\\ 7-\\sqrt[3]{{t}^{3}}\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{2t}+6t\\sqrt[3]{2t} & = \\\\ 7-t\\sqrt[3]{2t}+6t\\sqrt[3]{2t} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-t\\sqrt[3]{2t}+6t\\sqrt[3]{2t} & = \\\\ 7+(-t+6t)\\sqrt[3]{2t} & = \\\\ 7+5t\\sqrt[3]{2t} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502018 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502139 1069 "q-143033" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502139 1071 "A sample of the different prices of a given product in $64$ randomly selected stores in a given state yields a mean price of $$37.15$ with a sample standard deviation of $$2.00.$  Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean price of the product  $\\mu $ across the state. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $62$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.670\\,$ | $\\,1.999\\,$ | $\\,2.388\\,$ | $\\,2.657\\,$ |\n| $63$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.387\\,$ | $\\,2.656\\,$ |\n| $64$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.386\\,$ | $\\,2.655\\,$ |" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502139 1072 #uuid "b9cb4d01-534f-524c-85ee-ddefd795826f" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502139 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=$37.15,\\,n=64,\\,s=$2.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{99,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{63,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=64-1=63$ degrees of freedom. \n\n![](images/43/43ddb73bfc30887b0de7b7debf98407dbcea330ebe55c485982d5c05c0681c9a.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{63,0.05}=1.669:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $62$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.670\\,$ | $\\,1.999\\,$ | $\\,2.388\\,$ | $\\,2.657\\,$ |\n| $63$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $1.669$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.387\\,$ | $\\,2.656\\,$ |\n| $64$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.386\\,$ | $\\,2.655\\,$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37.15 & \\pm 1.669\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{64}}\\text{ dollars} \\\\ 37.15 & \\pm 0.417\\text{ dollars}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502003 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502140 1069 "q-109537" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502140 1071 "Find, in point-slope form, the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-3,-1)$ and $(4,2).$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502140 1072 #uuid "bd240e88-fbe1-5844-ad36-74f8bed7e2a0" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502140 1074 "We know that the line passes through the point $(-3,-1),$ so we can use $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-3,-1)$ in our point-slope formula. All that remains is to compute $m,$ and we can do this using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{2-(-1)}{4-(-3)}=\\frac{2+1}{4+3}=\\frac{3}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=\\frac{3}{7}$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-3,-1)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-1) & =\\frac{3}{7}(x-(-3)) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{3}{7}(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502061 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502141 1069 "q-147482" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502141 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502141 1071 "A smartphone app has $500\\,000$ users, $150\\,000$ of which have a paid account. If an app developer randomly samples $300$ users, find a normal approximation for the probability that between $72$ and $90$ (inclusive) users in the sample have a paid account. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0091\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0089\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0087\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0119\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0116\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0113\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0154\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0150\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0146\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4602\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4522\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5000\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5040\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5080\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5398\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5438\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5478\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502141 1072 #uuid "c30b7d81-9bb5-5e5f-938c-eb285e73259b" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502141 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=300,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{150\\,000}{500\\,000}=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=300(0.3)=90>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=300(1-0.3)=210>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=300$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{300}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0007).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If between $72$ and $90$ of users in the sample have a paid account, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{72}{300}\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le \\frac{90}{300} \\\\ 0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p} & \\le 0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3) & =P(\\frac{0.24-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0007}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.3-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0007}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.27\\le Z\\le 0) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0)-P(Z\\le -2.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0)=0.5000,\\,Φ(-2.27)=0.0116.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.24\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.3) & =Φ(0)-Φ(-2.27) \\\\  & =0.5000-0.0116 \\\\  & =0.4884.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502000 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502142 1069 "q-336503" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502142 1071 "A fertilizer tank for an irrigation system can hold up to $210\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $90\\,\\text{gal}$ of fertilizer solution with $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved fertilizer. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502142 1072 #uuid "c3e80580-6c31-591c-a6d6-df75a9aede3a" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502142 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank minus the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of fertilizer enters the tank at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *fertilizer* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =9\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which fertilizer exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which fertilizer leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $90\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $18\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=90+18t-12t=6(15+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of fertilizer in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *fertilizer* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{6(15+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{15+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =9-\\frac{2Q}{15+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{15+t}\\,Q=9.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(15+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|15+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(15+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(15+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(15+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(15+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{15+t}\\,Q) & =(15+t)^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\ (15+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(15+t)Q & =9(15+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(15+t)^{2}) & =9(15+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(15+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 9(15+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(15+t)^{2} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(15+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(15+t)+\\frac{C}{(15+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $18\\,\\text{lb}$ of fertilizer. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=18,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =18 \\\\ 3(15+0)+\\frac{C}{(15+0)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ 45+\\frac{C}{(15)^{2}} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{15}^{2}} & =-27 \\\\ C & =-27(15)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(15+t)-\\frac{27(15)^{2}}{(15+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =3(15+t)-27{(\\frac{15}{15+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $210\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=210$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =210 \\\\ 90+18{t}_{o}-12{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 90+6{t}_{o} & =210 \\\\ 6{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =20\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of fertilizer in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(20) & =3(15+20)-27{(\\frac{15}{15+20})}^{2} \\\\  & =105-27{(\\frac{15}{35})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈100.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502060 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502143 1069 "q-130593" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502143 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x),$ where $f(x)=3x-3?$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502143 1072 #uuid "c840c5f3-0fd7-561e-88b0-406ba1f39b38" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502143 1074 "Recall that the graph of $y=3x-3$ is a straight line, and two distinct points can determine any straight line.\n\nSo, given any two distinct values of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$-values by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=3x-3.$\nFor example,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=3(0)-3=-3,$ which gives the point $(0,-3),$ and\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=3(1)-3=0,$ which gives the point $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This gives the following graph.\n\n![](images/58/58731786b9a03b6b76a0a9fa0b91563d2b4a098c8a0e8b1d5ace5690d3ea2913.png)" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502035 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502144 1069 "q-130598" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502144 1071 "Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x),$ where $f(x)=2-\\frac{1}{2}x?$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502144 1072 #uuid "ca565001-9394-5e0a-81d1-cb1231ca5afa" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502144 1074 "Recall that the graph of $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is a straight line, and two distinct points can determine any straight line.\n\nSo, given any two distinct values of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$-values by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\nFor example,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}(0)=2,$ which gives the point $(0,2),$ and\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}(2)=1,$ which gives the point $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This gives the following graph.\n\n![](images/14/14ca58b7ec006bba9465672bb00da30e2539125f66a460ad5b5ea38521a836f5.png)" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502006 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502145 1069 "q-337315" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502145 1071 "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=C,$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502145 1072 #uuid "cc13bee1-bf2a-51a5-a80d-77b695b1c60b" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502145 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(C) \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)})}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502027 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502146 1069 "q-52392" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502146 1071 "$2\\sqrt{y}+7\\sqrt{y}=$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502146 1072 #uuid "cf46068e-803c-5f09-a183-8df712f591aa" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502146 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt{y}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{y}+7\\sqrt{y} & = \\\\ (2+7)\\sqrt{y} & = \\\\ 9\\sqrt{y} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502053 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502147 1069 "q-357219" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502147 1071 "Over $6$ days, $$14.70$ is deducted from a transit card balance due to daily fares of equal size. What is the change in the balance each day?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502147 1072 #uuid "cf763c67-fa75-5f72-8661-230a30b13c07" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502147 1074 "Since the balance decreases by $$14.70$ over $6$ days, the total change is $-$14.70.$\n\nThis change occurs evenly over $6$ days, so we divide the total change by $6:$\n\n$$\n-14.70÷6\n$$\n\nEvaluating the quotient, we get\n\n$$\n-14.70÷6=-2.45.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in the balance each day is $-$2.45.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502016 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502148 1069 "q-339403" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502148 1071 "A wastewater treatment basin initially holds $320\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid containing $2.2\\,\\text{kg}$ of dissolved neutralizing agent.\nA feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the agent is pumped into the basin at a rate of $8\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed liquid is drained out at a rate of $4\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nFind, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of agent in the basin $5$ minutes after pumping begins." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502148 1072 #uuid "d0340e70-b947-5b25-8e24-a63ff0f87f38" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502148 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of agent in the basin at time $t,$ in minutes. Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t.$ This is equal to the rate at which agent enters the tank minus the rate at which agent leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which agent enters the basin.\n\nNote that a feed solution containing $0.05\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of the agent enters the basin at a rate of $8\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *agent* enters the basin is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.05\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which agent exits the basin.\n\nTo find the rate at which agent leaves the basin, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the basin at time $t.$\n\nThe basin initially contains $320\\,\\text{L}$ of liquid; a solution enters at $8\\,\\text{L/min}$ and leaves at $4\\,\\text{L/min}.$\nAs a result, the volume of liquid in the basin after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n320+8t-4t=(320+4t)\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of agent in the basin, the *concentration* of the mixed solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\text{kg/L}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed liquid leaves at a rate of $4\\,\\text{L/min},$ *agent* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(4\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(4\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{320+4t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{80+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0.4-\\frac{Q}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80+t}Q=0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1/(80+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|80+t|} \\\\  & =80+t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=80+t$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(80+t)\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{80+t}Q) & =(80+t)\\cdot 0.4 \\\\ (80+t)\\frac{dQ}{dt}+Q & =0.4(80+t) \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(80+t)) & =0.4(80+t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(80+t))\\,dt & =\\int 0.4(80+t)\\,dt \\\\ Q(80+t) & =0.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(80+t)^{2}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =0.2(80+t)+\\frac{C}{80+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the basin initially contains $2.2\\,\\text{kg}$ of agent. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=2.2,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =2.2 \\\\ 0.2(80+0)+\\frac{C}{80+0} & =2.2 \\\\ 16+\\frac{C}{80} & =2.2 \\\\ \\frac{C}{80} & =-13.8 \\\\ C & =-1104\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =0.2(80+t)-\\frac{1104}{80+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,  the amount of agent in the basin $5$ minutes after pumping begins is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(5) & =0.2(80+5)-\\frac{1104}{80+5} \\\\  & =17-\\frac{1104}{85} \\\\  & ≈4.01\\,\\text{kg},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501979 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502149 1069 "q-142921" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502149 1071 "The body mass index (BMI) of Dr. Lee's patients is normally distributed. A random sample of $9$ patients yields a mean BMI of $25.5\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}$ with a standard deviation of $2.6\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}.$ Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean BMI $\\mu $ among all of Dr. Lee's patients. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $\\,1.860\\,$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502149 1072 #uuid "d3b129d2-0cd5-58e9-b5e8-9079b387fb53" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502149 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=25.5\\,\\,n=9,\\,s=2.6\\,.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{8,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{8,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=9-1=8$ degrees of freedom. \n\n![](images/ce/ce7d5fc9bbf2c9e28a0862247e6f762cad8a718401828070e96041e7f82b1891.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{8,0.05}=1.860:$   \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $1.860$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 25.5\\pm 1.860\\cdot \\frac{2.6}{\\sqrt{9}}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2} \\\\  & 25.5\\pm 1.612\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502028 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502150 1069 "q-109573" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502150 1071 "Calculate the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-5,3)$ and $(-1,-1).$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502150 1072 #uuid "d43f31da-b163-56de-90b2-998690e787ff" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502150 1074 "First, we will construct the point-slope formula for the line. Then, we will simplify the point-slope formula into slope-intercept form.\n\nWe start by computing the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{-1-3}{-1-(-5)}=\\frac{-4}{4}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=-1$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-5,3)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-(x-(-5)) \\\\ y-3 & =-(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the equation into slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-(x+5) \\\\ y-3 & =-x-5 \\\\ y & =-x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502032 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502151 1069 "q-146816" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502151 1071 "For which of the following situations can the distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the population proportion?\n\nI. A sample of size $n=100$ from a population of size $N=500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nII. A sample of size $n=60$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic.\n\nIII. A sample of size $n=40$ from a population of size $N=1\\,500$ in which $90%$ of the population has a particular characteristic." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502151 1072 #uuid "d6efb51f-1cc6-56d3-90b2-77b3cfdb82a3" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502151 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $n\\le 5%\\cdot N$ (the sample size is less than (or equal to) $5%$ of the population size),\n\n- $np>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements have the characteristic), and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5$ (more than $5$ sample elements do not have the characteristic).\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =100, \\\\ 5%\\cdot N & =0.05\\cdot 500 \\\\  & =25 \\\\  & <100.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  the sample size is more than $5%$ of the population size, we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =60 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,500 \\\\  & =75\\,✓ \\\\ np & =60(0.90) \\\\  & =54 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60(1-0.90) \\\\  & =6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\n- In situation III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =40 \\\\  & \\le 5%\\cdot N \\\\  & =0.05\\cdot 1\\,500 \\\\  & =75\\,✓ \\\\ np & =40(0.9) \\\\  & =36 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =40(1-0.9) \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $n(1-p)≯5,$ we *cannot* approximate the distribution of $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ using the given model.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501989 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502152 1069 "q-357085" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502152 1071 "The temperature of a liquid decreases by $\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute for $\\frac{8}{3}$ minutes. What is the total change in temperature?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502152 1072 #uuid "d71152b6-16b1-5e4e-a992-55dfd11f6205" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502152 1074 "The temperature decreases by $\\frac{3}{4}{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ each minute.\n\nSince the temperature decreases, the change each minute is $-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nThe change occurs for $\\frac{8}{3}$ minutes, so the total change is\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}\\times (-\\frac{3}{4}).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{3}\\times (-\\frac{3}{4})=-\\frac{8\\times 3}{3\\times 4}=-\\frac{8}{4}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in temperature is $-2{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502029 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502153 1069 "q-44192" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502153 1071 "![](images/db/dbf33e6d3a9e1efb4d0217f9076b0eb1381ea7d7fa9efa7ef7759b1c0e20175c.png)\n\nThe line shown above passes through the points $(0,3)$ and $(2,0).$ What is the equation of the line?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502153 1072 #uuid "d734e86c-2f4c-5a69-bdfa-2077440bd884" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502153 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,3).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=3.$\n\nWe can calculate the slope of the line by choosing two points on the line and applying the slope formula. The line passes through the points $(0,3)$ and $(2,0),$ so we calculate its slope as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-3}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and the $y$-intercept $b=3$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502038 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502154 1069 "q-146972" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502154 1071 "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=75$ from a population where $45%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $50%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502154 1072 #uuid "d98ad49e-5d4d-5c1b-b380-745357c60a55" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502154 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=75,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.45.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=75(0.45)=33.75>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=75(1-0.45)=41.25>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=75$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,\\frac{0.45(1-0.45)}{75}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.45,0.0033).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.5).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.5) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{0.5-0.45}{\\sqrt{0.0033}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le 0.87) \\\\  & =Φ(0.87)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502062 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502155 1069 "q-357210" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502155 1071 "A diver descends a total of $32.5\\,\\text{m}$ over $5$ minutes at a constant rate. What is the change in elevation each minute?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502155 1072 #uuid "e034c9b1-b463-56a2-afff-761ad637fb35" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502155 1074 "Since the diver's elevation decreases by $32.5\\,\\text{m}$ over $5$ minutes, the total change is $-32.5\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nThis change occurs evenly over $5$ minutes, so we divide the total change by $5:$\n\n$$\n-32.5÷5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the quotient, we get\n\n$$\n-32.5÷5=-6.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change in elevation each minute is $-6.5\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502015 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502156 1069 "q-357194" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502156 1071 "A hot air balloon descends $\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{m}$ each second. What is the total change in altitude after $\\frac{9}{7}$ seconds?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502156 1072 #uuid "e8734cff-4115-50f2-9d56-00580f475192" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502156 1074 "The hot air balloon descends $\\frac{7}{3}\\,\\text{m}$ each second.\n\nSince the altitude decreases, the change each second is $-\\frac{7}{3}.$\n\nThe descent lasts $\\frac{9}{7}$ seconds, so the total change in altitude is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{7}\\times (-\\frac{7}{3}).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{7}\\times (-\\frac{7}{3})=-\\frac{9\\times 7}{7\\times 3}=-\\frac{9}{3}=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total change in altitude is $-3\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501998 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502157 1069 "q-336840" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502157 1071 "A $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity water tank initially holds $7\\,200\\,\\text{mL}$ of water containing $210\\,\\text{g}$ of dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502157 1072 #uuid "ea65973e-51d0-5969-ad2d-3fe183a87ba6" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502157 1074 "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $7\\,200\\,\\text{mL}$ of water; clean water enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed water leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,200+90t-60t=30(240+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{240+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{240+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{240+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{240+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|240+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(240+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $210\\,\\text{g}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=210,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =210 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(240+0)^{2}} & =210 \\\\ A & =210(240)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{210(240)^{2}}{(240+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =210{(\\frac{240}{240+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=10\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 30(240+{t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 240+{t}_{o} & =350 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =110\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(110) & =210{(\\frac{240}{240+110})}^{2} \\\\  & =210{(\\frac{24}{35})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈98.74\\,\\text{g},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502019 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502158 1069 "q-336490" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502158 1071 "A processing tank initially holds $750\\,\\text{L}$ of water containing $900\\,\\text{g}$ of dissolved sugar.\nA solution containing $18\\,\\text{g/L}$ of dissolved sugar is pumped into the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of sugar $Q$ in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502158 1072 #uuid "ee7a7310-6a9a-5bb5-9fe8-376a138abe62" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502158 1074 "Since $Q$ is the mass of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a water solution containing $18\\,\\text{g/L}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =108\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $750\\,\\text{L}$ of water.\n\n- A solution enters the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $6t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed water solution leaves the tank at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $6t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of water in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n750+6t-6t=750\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the *concentration* of the water solution at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{L/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{750}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{125}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =108-\\frac{Q}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{125}Q=108.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502005 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502159 1069 "q-109542" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502159 1071 "Find, in point-slope form, the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-1,4)$ and $(5,2).$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502159 1072 #uuid "f088ab98-ea37-5733-acee-5703f82f16fe" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502159 1074 "We know that the line passes through the point $(-1,4),$ so we can use $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,4)$ in our point-slope formula. All that remains is to compute $m,$ and we can do this using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{2-4}{5-(-1)}=\\frac{-2}{6}=-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $m=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,4)$ into the point-slope formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502160 1069 "e-22713" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502160 1071 "A customer opens a bank account with a balance of ![](images/b6/b6d32477e84b569c63644d22eac5c4ac7628d3513fa649efe3b53af4dd46bd63.svg). The account allows overdrafts, and the customer withdraws ![](images/3f/3fb4b29875dcbc25b7a9836d939831543811af3f8b7959c34bbe2ef7c5739187.svg) each day for ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) days. What is the account balance after ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) days?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502160 1072 #uuid "f100ae8f-6865-5741-a125-4d55e90e2e43" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502160 1074 "The bank balance decreases by ![](images/3f/3fb4b29875dcbc25b7a9836d939831543811af3f8b7959c34bbe2ef7c5739187.svg) each day.\n\nSince the change is a decrease and occurs for ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) days, the total change can be written as repeated subtraction of ![](images/34/3474a36d6b0c9f191464fa9f990df13762af9a0c6b5e8138242a195cc33120e6.svg)\n\n![](images/f5/f5fbbe1aa003f39beaa1c8bbc83d1ba09f5588bb3e2a061287093e214f2ffb78.svg)\n\nRepeated subtraction can also be written using multiplication:\n\n![](images/4d/4db89374d470b00b0463b35e087efbb5ebfd8cbc0e0a408a9bfaf052cd9c5a32.svg)\n\nEvaluating the product, we get\n\n![](images/0a/0a1462b31c3a16cb37e282f254ad252b061aff0534e9bdf5972144d7de7e5a8a.svg)\n\nTherefore, the account balance after ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) days is ![](images/27/27f2d0115060366cb914179f4e2e6e9b0f9ddb29a6530984a105d7a0f5f29f71.svg)" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501993 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502161 1069 "q-336618" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502161 1071 "A tank initially holds $500\\,\\text{L}$ of a brine (salt-water) solution containing $18\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt.\nA brine solution containing $0.12\\,\\text{kg}$ of salt per liter is poured into the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ while the completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ Which differential equation best describes the amount of salt $Q,$ in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after the brine begins to be added?" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502161 1072 #uuid "f1d959d4-e84b-537b-9d31-22a9bf3bca3a" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502161 1074 "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the tank minus the rate at which salt leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{kg}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a brine solution containing $0.12\\,\\text{kg/L}$ of salt enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.12\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.12\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =2.4\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the tank.\n\nNow, to find the rate at which salt leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of brine in the tank at time $t.$\n\n- The tank initially contains $500\\,\\text{L}$ of brine.\n\n- Brine enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have entered the tank.\n\n- The completely mixed brine leaves the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min}.$ So, after $t$ minutes, $20t\\,\\text{L}$ have left the tank.\n\nAs a result, the volume of brine in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\n500+20t-20t=500\\,\\text{L}.\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is the mass of salt in the tank, the *concentration* of the brine at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed brine leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{L/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{L}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{L}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\cancel{\\text{L}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{500}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{Q}{25}\\,\\frac{\\text{kg}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nFinally, the rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =2.4-\\frac{Q}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1}{25}Q=2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502047 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502162 1069 "q-337313" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502162 1071 "Determine the differential equation satisfied by the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2},$ where $C\\ne 0$ is a parameter." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502162 1072 #uuid "f66e36cd-9bf6-56e0-b87a-016d297333d7" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502162 1074 "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $\\frac{x}{y}={C}^{2}.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{x}{y}) & =\\frac{d}{dx}\\,({C}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{y-x{y}^{′}}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ y-x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{y}{x})}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Since $C\\ne 0,$ the given family has $x\\ne 0$ and $y\\ne 0,$ so both ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ are well-defined." 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502044 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502163 1069 "q-44391" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502163 1071 "The lines $y=\\frac{8}{5}x+9$ and $y=mx+3$ are parallel. The value of $m=$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502163 1072 #uuid "f9e5b1d4-9811-5c7c-998e-01070d311e7e" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502163 1074 "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nBoth lines are in slope-intercept form, so we can pick out the slope of each equation by looking at the $x$-coefficient.\n\n- The slope of the line $y=\\frac{8}{5}x+9$ is $\\frac{8}{5}.$\n\n- The slope of the line $y=mx+3$ is $m.$\n\nBecause the slopes must be the same, we must have $m=\\frac{8}{5}.$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502057 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502164 1069 "q-147479" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502164 1071 "A dried apricot producer knows that $70%$ of apricots weigh more than $40$ g. If the factory randomly samples $350$ apricots, find a normal approximation for the probability that fewer than $224$ apricots weigh more than $40$ g. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502164 1072 #uuid "fff0efc8-e9b9-52d5-b287-771e1cbfdc28" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502164 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=350,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.7.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=350(0.7)=245>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=350(1-0.7)=105>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=350$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{350}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,0.0006).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $224$ apricots in the sample weigh more than $40$ g, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}<\\frac{224}{350}=0.64.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.64).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.64) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.64-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.0006}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-2.45) \\\\  & =Φ(-2.45).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502165 1058 #edb/ref 17592186502172 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502165 1062 #uuid "06ab3711-3d4c-5c2c-8653-bca368f0f08b" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502165 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069048 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502166 1058 #edb/ref 17592186502167 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502166 1062 #uuid "07cd0f42-e686-5aa2-b3b0-7e07e394a2c4" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502166 1063 #edb/ref 17592186502153 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502167 1058 #edb/ref 17592186502214 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502167 1062 #uuid "0c73f501-a6b3-5d8e-af7b-d2238fefcc83" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502167 1063 #edb/ref 17592186101258 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502168 1058 #edb/ref 17592186502173 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502168 1062 #uuid "102a9be1-877b-502d-8f9a-b0b5673f06b0" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502168 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111380 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502169 1058 #edb/ref 17592186502203 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502169 1062 #uuid "15ba228c-73d6-5b27-b06b-8123523bb9ba" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502169 1063 #edb/ref 17592186402002 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186502170 1058 #edb/ref 17592186502196 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186